  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Phase 1: Project Foundation & Flight Prototype\n\nYou are building **STARFALL**, a browser-based 3D sci-fi arcade space combat game.\n\nYour task is to create the project foundation and implement a fully playable Phase 1 flight prototype.\n\nDo not build a static mockup. The result must be an actual interactive 3D game that runs in the Google AI Studio preview.\n\n## 1. Product Vision\n\nSTARFALL is a cinematic, fast-paced, third-person space fighter combat game.\n\nThe complete planned game will feature:\n\n- Multiple playable spaceships.\n- Different enemy bot personalities and combat behaviors.\n- Five progressively difficult combat missions.\n- Performance-based ranking and progression.\n- Unlockable spaceships.\n- Cinematic space environments.\n- Responsive arcade flight controls.\n- A polished futuristic HUD and game interface.\n\nThe initial release is single-player and browser-based.\n\nNo backend, multiplayer, accounts, or paid services are required.\n\n## 2. Technology Stack\n\nUse:\n\n- React\n- TypeScript\n- Three.js\n- React Three Fiber\n- Drei\n- Web Audio API\n\nUse compatible, stable npm packages.\n\nKeep the architecture modular and maintainable.\n\nSeparate the following systems:\n\n1. Game engine and runtime.\n2. Flight controller.\n3. Camera controller.\n4. Spaceship models and configuration.\n5. Input management.\n6. Rendering and visual effects.\n7. UI and HUD.\n8. Audio management.\n9. Game state management.\n\nImportant performance requirement:\n\nDo not use React state updates for every frame of gameplay.\n\nUse Three.js objects, refs, and a frame-update system for real-time movement and physics. Use React state for menus, HUD updates, and other interface state.\n\nUse delta time for all movement and physics calculations.\n\n## 3. Phase 1 Scope \342\200\224 Fully Playable Flight Prototype\n\nBuild the following features now.\n\n### A. 3D Space Environment\n\nCreate a visually impressive 3D space environment.\n\nInclude:\n\n- Deep space background.\n- Procedural star field.\n- Distant planets.\n- Nebula-like visual effects.\n- Atmospheric lighting.\n- Subtle environmental depth.\n\nThe environment should feel spacious and cinematic.\n\nAvoid an empty black scene with a few stars.\n\nUse procedural geometry and shaders where appropriate.\n\nDo not depend on unavailable external assets.\n\n### B. Player Spaceship\n\nCreate a detailed futuristic 3D player fighter.\n\nDesign requirements:\n\n- Sleek aerodynamic science-fiction silhouette.\n- Distinct wings and engine structures.\n- Metallic materials.\n- Glowing cyan or blue engine lights.\n- Visible engine exhaust.\n- Recognizable front, rear, and side profiles.\n\nUse actual 3D geometry.\n\nDo not use a flat image or simple primitive as the final spaceship.\n\nThe spaceship must have a clear forward direction and appropriate local coordinate system.\n\n### C. Flight Controller\n\nImplement arcade-style flight physics.\n\nDefault controls:\n\n| Input | Action |\n|---|---|\n| Mouse X | Yaw |\n| Mouse Y | Pitch |\n| A | Roll left |\n| D | Roll right |\n| W | Accelerate forward |\n| S | Decelerate / reverse |\n| Shift | Boost |\n| Esc | Pause and release pointer lock |\n\nMouse movement must use relative pointer movement through the Pointer Lock API during gameplay.\n\nThe player should be able to control the spaceship smoothly without the mouse cursor reaching screen boundaries.\n\nFlight requirements:\n\n- Smooth pitch and yaw.\n- Independent roll control.\n- Responsive directional control.\n- Adjustable mouse sensitivity.\n- Smooth acceleration.\n- Momentum and controlled inertia.\n- Forward and reverse movement.\n- Configurable maximum speed.\n- Boost speed multiplier.\n- Boost energy consumption and regeneration.\n\nThe controls should feel responsive and intuitive.\n\nDo not implement realistic atmospheric aerodynamics.\n\nThis is a space arcade game.\n\nThe ship should be capable of aggressive turns and fast directional changes.\n\n### D. Boost System\n\nImplement a working boost mechanic.\n\nWhile Shift is held:\n\n- Increase maximum movement speed.\n- Increase engine glow.\n- Increase engine exhaust intensity.\n- Add subtle speed-line effects.\n- Increase camera field of view.\n- Add a slight camera effect to communicate acceleration.\n\nUse a boost energy meter.\n\nBoost energy should regenerate when boost is inactive.\n\n### E. Third-Person Chase Camera\n\nImplement a polished third-person camera.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Follow behind the player spaceship.\n- Smooth positional interpolation.\n- Smooth rotational tracking.\n- Adjustable camera distance.\n- Adjustable camera sensitivity.\n- Subtle camera lag during aggressive turns.\n- Dynamic field of view during boost.\n- Appropriate camera response during rolling.\n\nThe camera should make the player feel like they are piloting a fast fighter.\n\nAvoid excessive camera shaking.\n\nDo not allow the spaceship to disappear from view during normal flight.\n\n### F. Flight HUD\n\nCreate a professional sci-fi HUD overlay.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Current speed.\n- Hull integrity.\n- Boost energy.\n- Center aiming reticle.\n- Flight status.\n- Keyboard control hints.\n\nUse a dark transparent interface with cyan and electric blue highlights.\n\nThe HUD should remain readable against bright and dark backgrounds.\n\nUse clean typography and subtle animations.\n\nDo not cover too much of the gameplay screen.\n\n### G. Main Menu\n\nCreate an initial main menu with:\n\n- STARFALL logo and title.\n- Play.\n- Hangar.\n- Settings.\n\nFor Phase 1:\n\n- Play starts the flight prototype.\n- Hangar can display a preview of the planned spaceship selection system.\n- Settings must support mouse sensitivity and audio volume.\n- Escape pauses gameplay and provides a resume option.\n\nDo not create nonfunctional buttons.\n\nIf a feature is not implemented yet, clearly mark it as coming soon.\n\n### H. Audio\n\nUse Web Audio API or suitable local audio assets.\n\nImplement:\n\n- Engine sound.\n- Boost sound.\n- UI interaction sounds.\n\nAudio must respect browser autoplay restrictions.\n\nStart audio after the player interacts with the game.\n\nProvide a master volume control.\n\n## 4. Future Architecture Requirements\n\nThe complete MVP will eventually include three playable spaceships:\n\n**Interceptor**\n- High speed.\n- High maneuverability.\n- Low hull integrity.\n\n**Striker**\n- Balanced speed.\n- Balanced maneuverability.\n- Balanced hull integrity.\n\n**Destroyer**\n- High hull integrity.\n- High weapon damage.\n- Lower maneuverability.\n\nEach spaceship must eventually have a unique 3D model and independent configuration.\n\nDesign the architecture so these ships can be added without rewriting the flight controller.\n\nThe future combat system will include:\n\n- Player weapons.\n- Enemy projectiles.\n- Collision detection.\n- Enemy health.\n- Four bot personalities: Aggressor, Flanker, Defender, and Ace.\n- Target acquisition and tracking.\n- Enemy evasion.\n- Combat scoring.\n\nThe future mission system will include five missions with increasing difficulty.\n\nMission results will use S, A, B, and C ranks.\n\nProgression will depend on performance.\n\nUnlocks and progress will eventually use browser LocalStorage.\n\nDo not implement the full combat or progression systems in this phase. Prepare the architecture for them.\n\n## 5. Visual Direction\n\nThe visual direction is:\n\n**Cinematic Sci-Fi / Premium Space Combat**\n\nUse:\n\n- Electric cyan.\n- Deep navy.\n- Dark metallic surfaces.\n- Glowing engine effects.\n- Cinematic lighting.\n- High-quality materials.\n- Atmospheric space environments.\n- Subtle holographic interface elements.\n\nAvoid:\n\n- Generic dashboard aesthetics.\n- Flat 2D spaceship illustrations.\n- Excessive gradients.\n- Oversized UI panels.\n- Cartoonish visual effects.\n- Unnecessary decorative elements.\n\nThe gameplay must remain the visual focus.\n\nAim for a polished commercial indie space game.\n\n## 6. Performance Requirements\n\nTarget 60 FPS on a typical modern desktop computer.\n\nImplement:\n\n- Delta-time-based movement.\n- Efficient rendering.\n- Reasonable polygon counts.\n- Reusable geometries and materials.\n- Efficient particle management.\n- Limited dynamic lighting.\n- Proper cleanup of Three.js resources.\n\nAvoid unnecessary per-frame allocations.\n\nThe game should remain responsive during extended play.\n\n## 7. Implementation Workflow\n\nFollow this order:\n\n1. Inspect the project environment.\n2. Establish the application structure.\n3. Install required dependencies.\n4. Implement the 3D scene.\n5. Implement the player spaceship.\n6. Implement flight controls.\n7. Implement the chase camera.\n8. Implement boost.\n9. Implement the HUD.\n10. Implement the menu and settings.\n11. Add audio.\n12. Test the complete flight experience.\n\nWork directly in the Google AI Studio project.\n\nDo not stop after creating the initial interface.\n\n## 8. Testing and Acceptance Criteria\n\nPhase 1 is complete only when:\n\n- The application starts successfully.\n- The player can enter gameplay.\n- A real 3D spaceship is visible.\n- Mouse-controlled yaw and pitch work.\n- A/D roll works independently.\n- W/S movement works.\n- Shift boost works.\n- Boost energy regenerates.\n- The chase camera follows correctly.\n- Pointer Lock works correctly.\n- Escape pauses the game and releases the cursor.\n- The HUD updates correctly.\n- Mouse sensitivity settings work.\n- Audio controls work.\n- The game can be resumed after pausing.\n- No critical console errors remain.\n\nTest actual gameplay in the preview.\n\nDo not claim a feature works unless it has been tested.\n\n## 9. Important Development Rules\n\n- Prioritize functional gameplay over decorative UI.\n- Do not build the entire game in one step.\n- Do not introduce unnecessary backend services.\n- Do not use paid APIs.\n- Do not use placeholder buttons for features that should work in Phase 1.\n- Do not replace working systems without a clear reason.\n- Keep code readable and modular.\n- Make reasonable implementation decisions independently.\n- If a technical limitation occurs, explain it and implement the best practical alternative.\n\nAt the end, provide:\n\n1. Summary of implemented features.\n2. Project architecture.\n3. Dependencies installed.\n4. Tests performed and their results.\n5. Known issues.\n6. Recommended next development phase.\n\n**Your immediate objective: deliver a polished, genuinely playable 3D spaceship flight prototype. The quality of flight controls and camera movement is the highest priority.**"
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Phase 1: Project Foundation & Flight Prototype\n\nYou are building **STARFALL**, a browser-based 3D sci-fi arcade space combat game.\n\nYour task is to create the project foundation and implement a fully playable Phase 1 flight prototype.\n\nDo not build a static mockup. The result must be an actual interactive 3D game that runs in the Google AI Studio preview.\n\n## 1. Product Vision\n\nSTARFALL is a cinematic, fast-paced, third-person space fighter combat game.\n\nThe complete planned game will feature:\n\n- Multiple playable spaceships.\n- Different enemy bot personalities and combat behaviors.\n- Five progressively difficult combat missions.\n- Performance-based ranking and progression.\n- Unlockable spaceships.\n- Cinematic space environments.\n- Responsive arcade flight controls.\n- A polished futuristic HUD and game interface.\n\nThe initial release is single-player and browser-based.\n\nNo backend, multiplayer, accounts, or paid services are required.\n\n## 2. Technology Stack\n\nUse:\n\n- React\n- TypeScript\n- Three.js\n- React Three Fiber\n- Drei\n- Web Audio API\n\nUse compatible, stable npm packages.\n\nKeep the architecture modular and maintainable.\n\nSeparate the following systems:\n\n1. Game engine and runtime.\n2. Flight controller.\n3. Camera controller.\n4. Spaceship models and configuration.\n5. Input management.\n6. Rendering and visual effects.\n7. UI and HUD.\n8. Audio management.\n9. Game state management.\n\nImportant performance requirement:\n\nDo not use React state updates for every frame of gameplay.\n\nUse Three.js objects, refs, and a frame-update system for real-time movement and physics. Use React state for menus, HUD updates, and other interface state.\n\nUse delta time for all movement and physics calculations.\n\n## 3. Phase 1 Scope \342\200\224 Fully Playable Flight Prototype\n\nBuild the following features now.\n\n### A. 3D Space Environment\n\nCreate a visually impressive 3D space environment.\n\nInclude:\n\n- Deep space background.\n- Procedural star field.\n- Distant planets.\n- Nebula-like visual effects.\n- Atmospheric lighting.\n- Subtle environmental depth.\n\nThe environment should feel spacious and cinematic.\n\nAvoid an empty black scene with a few stars.\n\nUse procedural geometry and shaders where appropriate.\n\nDo not depend on unavailable external assets.\n\n### B. Player Spaceship\n\nCreate a detailed futuristic 3D player fighter.\n\nDesign requirements:\n\n- Sleek aerodynamic science-fiction silhouette.\n- Distinct wings and engine structures.\n- Metallic materials.\n- Glowing cyan or blue engine lights.\n- Visible engine exhaust.\n- Recognizable front, rear, and side profiles.\n\nUse actual 3D geometry.\n\nDo not use a flat image or simple primitive as the final spaceship.\n\nThe spaceship must have a clear forward direction and appropriate local coordinate system.\n\n### C. Flight Controller\n\nImplement arcade-style flight physics.\n\nDefault controls:\n\n| Input | Action |\n|---|---|\n| Mouse X | Yaw |\n| Mouse Y | Pitch |\n| A | Roll left |\n| D | Roll right |\n| W | Accelerate forward |\n| S | Decelerate / reverse |\n| Shift | Boost |\n| Esc | Pause and release pointer lock |\n\nMouse movement must use relative pointer movement through the Pointer Lock API during gameplay.\n\nThe player should be able to control the spaceship smoothly without the mouse cursor reaching screen boundaries.\n\nFlight requirements:\n\n- Smooth pitch and yaw.\n- Independent roll control.\n- Responsive directional control.\n- Adjustable mouse sensitivity.\n- Smooth acceleration.\n- Momentum and controlled inertia.\n- Forward and reverse movement.\n- Configurable maximum speed.\n- Boost speed multiplier.\n- Boost energy consumption and regeneration.\n\nThe controls should feel responsive and intuitive.\n\nDo not implement realistic atmospheric aerodynamics.\n\nThis is a space arcade game.\n\nThe ship should be capable of aggressive turns and fast directional changes.\n\n### D. Boost System\n\nImplement a working boost mechanic.\n\nWhile Shift is held:\n\n- Increase maximum movement speed.\n- Increase engine glow.\n- Increase engine exhaust intensity.\n- Add subtle speed-line effects.\n- Increase camera field of view.\n- Add a slight camera effect to communicate acceleration.\n\nUse a boost energy meter.\n\nBoost energy should regenerate when boost is inactive.\n\n### E. Third-Person Chase Camera\n\nImplement a polished third-person camera.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Follow behind the player spaceship.\n- Smooth positional interpolation.\n- Smooth rotational tracking.\n- Adjustable camera distance.\n- Adjustable camera sensitivity.\n- Subtle camera lag during aggressive turns.\n- Dynamic field of view during boost.\n- Appropriate camera response during rolling.\n\nThe camera should make the player feel like they are piloting a fast fighter.\n\nAvoid excessive camera shaking.\n\nDo not allow the spaceship to disappear from view during normal flight.\n\n### F. Flight HUD\n\nCreate a professional sci-fi HUD overlay.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Current speed.\n- Hull integrity.\n- Boost energy.\n- Center aiming reticle.\n- Flight status.\n- Keyboard control hints.\n\nUse a dark transparent interface with cyan and electric blue highlights.\n\nThe HUD should remain readable against bright and dark backgrounds.\n\nUse clean typography and subtle animations.\n\nDo not cover too much of the gameplay screen.\n\n### G. Main Menu\n\nCreate an initial main menu with:\n\n- STARFALL logo and title.\n- Play.\n- Hangar.\n- Settings.\n\nFor Phase 1:\n\n- Play starts the flight prototype.\n- Hangar can display a preview of the planned spaceship selection system.\n- Settings must support mouse sensitivity and audio volume.\n- Escape pauses gameplay and provides a resume option.\n\nDo not create nonfunctional buttons.\n\nIf a feature is not implemented yet, clearly mark it as coming soon.\n\n### H. Audio\n\nUse Web Audio API or suitable local audio assets.\n\nImplement:\n\n- Engine sound.\n- Boost sound.\n- UI interaction sounds.\n\nAudio must respect browser autoplay restrictions.\n\nStart audio after the player interacts with the game.\n\nProvide a master volume control.\n\n## 4. Future Architecture Requirements\n\nThe complete MVP will eventually include three playable spaceships:\n\n**Interceptor**\n- High speed.\n- High maneuverability.\n- Low hull integrity.\n\n**Striker**\n- Balanced speed.\n- Balanced maneuverability.\n- Balanced hull integrity.\n\n**Destroyer**\n- High hull integrity.\n- High weapon damage.\n- Lower maneuverability.\n\nEach spaceship must eventually have a unique 3D model and independent configuration.\n\nDesign the architecture so these ships can be added without rewriting the flight controller.\n\nThe future combat system will include:\n\n- Player weapons.\n- Enemy projectiles.\n- Collision detection.\n- Enemy health.\n- Four bot personalities: Aggressor, Flanker, Defender, and Ace.\n- Target acquisition and tracking.\n- Enemy evasion.\n- Combat scoring.\n\nThe future mission system will include five missions with increasing difficulty.\n\nMission results will use S, A, B, and C ranks.\n\nProgression will depend on performance.\n\nUnlocks and progress will eventually use browser LocalStorage.\n\nDo not implement the full combat or progression systems in this phase. Prepare the architecture for them.\n\n## 5. Visual Direction\n\nThe visual direction is:\n\n**Cinematic Sci-Fi / Premium Space Combat**\n\nUse:\n\n- Electric cyan.\n- Deep navy.\n- Dark metallic surfaces.\n- Glowing engine effects.\n- Cinematic lighting.\n- High-quality materials.\n- Atmospheric space environments.\n- Subtle holographic interface elements.\n\nAvoid:\n\n- Generic dashboard aesthetics.\n- Flat 2D spaceship illustrations.\n- Excessive gradients.\n- Oversized UI panels.\n- Cartoonish visual effects.\n- Unnecessary decorative elements.\n\nThe gameplay must remain the visual focus.\n\nAim for a polished commercial indie space game.\n\n## 6. Performance Requirements\n\nTarget 60 FPS on a typical modern desktop computer.\n\nImplement:\n\n- Delta-time-based movement.\n- Efficient rendering.\n- Reasonable polygon counts.\n- Reusable geometries and materials.\n- Efficient particle management.\n- Limited dynamic lighting.\n- Proper cleanup of Three.js resources.\n\nAvoid unnecessary per-frame allocations.\n\nThe game should remain responsive during extended play.\n\n## 7. Implementation Workflow\n\nFollow this order:\n\n1. Inspect the project environment.\n2. Establish the application structure.\n3. Install required dependencies.\n4. Implement the 3D scene.\n5. Implement the player spaceship.\n6. Implement flight controls.\n7. Implement the chase camera.\n8. Implement boost.\n9. Implement the HUD.\n10. Implement the menu and settings.\n11. Add audio.\n12. Test the complete flight experience.\n\nWork directly in the Google AI Studio project.\n\nDo not stop after creating the initial interface.\n\n## 8. Testing and Acceptance Criteria\n\nPhase 1 is complete only when:\n\n- The application starts successfully.\n- The player can enter gameplay.\n- A real 3D spaceship is visible.\n- Mouse-controlled yaw and pitch work.\n- A/D roll works independently.\n- W/S movement works.\n- Shift boost works.\n- Boost energy regenerates.\n- The chase camera follows correctly.\n- Pointer Lock works correctly.\n- Escape pauses the game and releases the cursor.\n- The HUD updates correctly.\n- Mouse sensitivity settings work.\n- Audio controls work.\n- The game can be resumed after pausing.\n- No critical console errors remain.\n\nTest actual gameplay in the preview.\n\nDo not claim a feature works unless it has been tested.\n\n## 9. Important Development Rules\n\n- Prioritize functional gameplay over decorative UI.\n- Do not build the entire game in one step.\n- Do not introduce unnecessary backend services.\n- Do not use paid APIs.\n- Do not use placeholder buttons for features that should work in Phase 1.\n- Do not replace working systems without a clear reason.\n- Keep code readable and modular.\n- Make reasonable implementation decisions independently.\n- If a technical limitation occurs, explain it and implement the best practical alternative.\n\nAt the end, provide:\n\n1. Summary of implemented features.\n2. Project architecture.\n3. Dependencies installed.\n4. Tests performed and their results.\n5. Known issues.\n6. Recommended next development phase.\n\n**Your immediate objective: deliver a polished, genuinely playable 3D spaceship flight prototype. The quality of flight controls and camera movement is the highest priority.**"
    }
  }
}
