  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Initiating Phase 2 Design**\n\nI\'m starting the Skill Check for Phase 2! Right now, my focus is frontend-design, specifically the FlightHUD. I\'m aiming to extend it with combat elements, like target brackets, health bars, hit markers, weapon status, damage vignettes, and combat stats. I\'m keeping the STARFALL aesthetic clean, so there\'s zero slop, no pill badges, and no backdrops.\n\n\n**Analyzing Mouse Aiming**\n\nI\'m now diving into the mouse aiming issue from Phase 1. Focusing on `InputManager.ts` and `FlightController.ts`, I\'m pinpointing the range restriction logic. Specifically, I\'m checking how the `e.movementX` and `e.movementY` values are being clamped within the `InputManager.ts` file. My goal is to understand precisely why the aiming felt limited and how to improve it in Section 2, without disrupting the existing codebase.\n\n\n**Unveiling Mouse Aim Control**\n\nI\'ve been examining the mouse input system in `FlightController.ts`. There are two significant observations. First, the code treats mouse movement as an immediate angular velocity impulse that decays back to zero, meaning that mouseDeltaX resets every frame. Second, the yaw and pitch input are clamped, resulting in a limited rotation angle on even a fast mouse sweep. This explains why aiming felt restricted, and it offers potential solutions.\n\n\n**Exploring Aim Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now considering the aiming behavior. It\'s clear the system\'s sensitivity is constrained to roughly 0.24 degrees per pixel, but even less due to the blending and clamping. A 300px mouse movement only results in a 45-degree rotation! I\'m now exploring how other space combat games, like *Everspace* or *Star Citizen*, approach expanded aiming envelopes using free-aim cursors or gimbaled aim offsets. Specifically, I\'m comparing virtual reticle aim vs. direct mouse deltas to create a smooth steering experience.\n\n\n**Refining Requirements Review**\n\nI\'m now deeply analyzing the user\'s specific demands for expanded mouse aiming. I\'ve re-read the detailed requirements in Section 2, carefully noting the emphasis on precision, responsiveness, and maintaining the current feel while dramatically increasing the effective aiming range. I\'m focusing on the delicate balance between expanding the aiming envelope and avoiding unwanted side effects, such as sensitivity issues or unpredictable ship behavior at extreme angles. I\'m taking special care to consider the existing Pointer Lock behavior.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Root Causes**\n\nI\'m now diagnosing the root causes of the restrictive aiming. I\'ve compiled a list of observations from the prompt: the aim range is too small, it needs to be expanded without changing global sensitivity, and it needs to preserve the current pointer lock behavior. The reticle must match the actual weapon firing direction. I\'ve identified the root causes: aggressive angular clamp and fast decay in `FlightController.ts`. I\'m now comparing this against the prompt\'s instructions.\n\n\n**Implementing Aim Enhancements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the specifics of the current aiming limitations. I\'ve pinpointed that angular velocity clamping drastically reduces mouse strokes, losing over 63% of motion. The immediate decay to zero on no-mouse frames further diminishes responsiveness. Moreover, the lack of a persistent virtual aim vector means players have to constantly move the mouse to sustain wide turns. The tight reticle clamping in `FlightHUD.tsx` and `CameraController.ts` contributes to the boxed-in aiming experience as well. I need to make sure I am implementing the dual-precision expanded aiming envelope.\n\n\n**Designing Aim Precision**\n\nI\'m now implementing the persistent virtual aim offset within `FlightController`, using an elliptical aiming envelope. This system includes a non-linear response curve, which I\'m testing to provide precision near the center and greater reach across the envelope. This will enhance the overall aiming experience, delivering a smooth transition between precision and maneuverability.\n\n\n**Considering Aim Techniques**\n\nI\'m now exploring combining instantaneous impulse with sustained envelope tracking. I\'m pondering a gentle micro-response for small flicks, a sustained turn rate within an aim zone, and soft auto-centering when the mouse stops. I\'m taking inspiration from *Everspace 2* and *Freelancer*, and considering a virtual mouse-aim ring. It will create a spacious zone where the ship\'s nose follows the cursor.\n\n\n**Evaluating Aim Integration**\n\nI\'m now grappling with integrating the enhanced aiming while preserving the Phase 1 feel. It\'s a critical UX issue! I\'m debating the implications of a classic FPS-style versus a Freelancer-style approach. I must avoid an unwanted shift in responsiveness. I need to design an intuitive system for the user who loved the initial feel.\n\n\n**Implementing Elastic Aim**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing the elastic aim-lead with an expanded angular envelope, as observed in modern arcade space shooters. My design incorporates direct ship steering for Phase 1\'s feel with non-linear acceleration. I\'m removing the aggressive clamps to accommodate larger mouse sweeps. I\'m working on a design where the weapon reticle leads the ship across a wide elliptical envelope, smoothly settling back to the ship\'s boresight.\n\n\n**Implementing Elastic Aim**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on implementing an elastic aim-lead with a wide angular envelope, as seen in modern arcade space shooters, for an enhanced sense of precision. I\'m focusing on preserving the initial feel while removing aggressive clamps and implementing non-linear acceleration for smooth and accurate weapon reticle movement. The weapon reticle will lead the ship through a wide elliptical envelope, gracefully settling back to the ship\'s boresight.\n\n\n**Refining Aim Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now implementing pure local-space quaternion integration, ensuring zero gimbal lock and smooth camera up-vector following ship local up, even at \302\26190\302\260 pitch. I\'m focusing on aligning the weapon firing direction with the aiming reticle, calculating it from the same 3D aim ray. I want to make sure it\'s mathematically sound! This should create rock-solid reticle accuracy and ensure the reticle aligns with the 3D position.\n\n\n**Verifying Aim Trajectory**\n\nI\'m now verifying the projectile trajectory math, ensuring the reticle is locked to the 3D firing line, especially during maneuvers. I\'m focusing on ensuring zero jitter and gimbal lock, while maintaining the spacious aiming envelope with the defined precision. I am focusing on the calculation: ship orientation `shipQuat` and player mouse aim offset `(aimYaw, aimPitch)`.\n\n**Initiating Phase 2 Design**\n\n**Analyzing Mouse Aiming**\n\n**Unveiling Mouse Aim Control**\n\n**Exploring Aim Dynamics**\n\n**Refining Requirements Review**\n\n**Diagnosing Root Causes**\n\n**Implementing Aim Enhancements**\n\n**Designing Aim Precision**\n\n**Considering Aim Techniques**\n\n**Implementing Aim Smoothing**\n\nI\'m now integrating the aim smoothing logic using a decoupled approach. My plan is to apply `(-deltaYaw, deltaPitch)` directly to `(aimYaw, aimPitch)` while softly clamping to the elliptical envelope. Simultaneously, the ship smoothly rotates towards `(aimYaw, aimPitch)` based on an angular turn rate. The goal is precise aiming, with a smooth and gentle response near the center, and a more aggressive turn rate when aimed further out. Every frame, I\'ll subtract the ship\'s rotation from the `(aimYaw, aimPitch)` for stable control and zero overshoot.\n\n\n**Evaluating Aim Integration**\n\nI\'m now integrating the decoupled aim smoothing. Small mouse movements result in precise reticle shifts, achieving surgical precision, but larger movements smoothly steer the ship. Roll inputs counter-rotate the aim, ensuring the reticle stays fixed, even during barrel rolls. The camera will smoothly follow the blended ship-forward and aim direction, maintaining an unclipped viewport. This fulfills the Section 2 & 6 requirements.\n\n\n**Integrating Combat Systems**\n\nI\'m now integrating AI combat elements into the system. I\'m focusing on the newly created `src/types/combat.ts` file, and I\'m developing a multiplayer-ready system. I\'m focusing on new entities such as `EntityId`, `Faction`, and `ProjectileState` to make sure all elements work as intended, and can handle various events like `DamageEvent`, `WeaponFireEvent`, and `DestructionEvent`. I am defining four bot personalities: `\'aggressor\'`, `\'flanker\'`, `\'defender\'`, and `\'ace\'`.\n\n\n**Defining Combat Features**\n\nI\'m now defining the complete combat system, using new entities in `src/types/combat.ts`. I am adding `EnemyEntity`, `CombatStats`, and `ProjectedTargetHUD` to build the required logic. I\'m also implementing the enemy behavior and weapon configurations within a reusable component. My goal is to build a foundation for AI encounters.\n\n\n**Defining Bot Configurations**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the bot personalities within `src/config/botPersonalities.ts`. I\'m defining distinct tactical profiles, considering difficulty scaling. Aggressor, Flanker, Defender, and Ace bots are now defined, with difficulty modifiers. Their behaviours include aggressive pursuit, flanking, defensive strafing, and evasive maneuvering with lead prediction.\n\n\n**Implementing Health and Pools**\n\nI\'m now integrating the `HealthSystem.ts` for reusable health management, damage validation, and returns structured `DamageResult`, ensuring positive damage and preventing damage on destroyed ships. Concurrently, I\'ve created the `ProjectilePool.ts` for a zero-allocation projectile simulation with a fixed capacity of 160 projectiles. I\'m focusing on the `spawn` and `update` functions.\n\n\n**Implementing Collision System**\n\nI\'m now integrating the swept-segment vs. sphere collision system in `CollisionSystem.ts`. This ensures fast and accurate projectile hit detection, even for high-velocity laser bolts. The use of a swept segment guarantees tunnel-proof collision detection. I\'ve also added ship-to-ship collision to prevent unwanted clipping.\n\n\n\n**Orchestrating Combat Flow**\n\nI\'m now integrating AI behavior within a battle manager, specifically, `CombatManager.ts`. It oversees projectile pooling, manages active enemies, tracks health, and handles weapon cooldowns. I\'m focusing on defining enemy behavior and weapon configurations within a reusable component. The system will handle projectile pools, combat stats, and firing events for a responsive combat experience.\n\n\n**Orchestrating Combat Flow**\n\nI\'m expanding the AI combat system with continuous waves of enemies, and I am incorporating new enemy starfighters, with Aggressor and Flanker personalities. The system is set up for infinite testing and a respawn/retry mechanism. I\'m focusing on firing, projectiles, swept collision detection, enemy AI, and combat telemetry, all while keeping React updates to a minimum.\n\n\n**Implementing Combat Feedback**\n\nI\'m now integrating instant combat feedback, creating non-repeating fire effects for muzzle flashes and explosions. I\'m focusing on draw call efficiency with instanced meshes, creating quick flashes without spawning new objects. I\'m also adding a reusable health system, which includes a projectile pool, all within the `src/types/game.ts` file. I\'m focused on the performance of the explosions, as well as the gameplay experience.\n\n\n**Integrating Combat Telemetry**\n\nI\'m now integrating weapon telemetry into the combat system. This includes adding a `targets` array to `FlightTelemetry`. Also, I\'ve added left mouse button tracking to `InputManager.ts` to support both pointer lock and canvas click firing. The focus is on ensuring the pointer lock doesn\'t interfere with mouse steering. Furthermore, I\'ve added a new aim-envelope system.\n\n\n**Refining Aim Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now integrating weapon telemetry into the combat system. Specifically, I\'m working on the `targets` array for `FlightTelemetry`, and added the mouse button tracking to `InputManager.ts` to allow both pointer lock and canvas firing. My goal is to ensure pointer lock doesn\'t interfere with mouse steering. Additionally, I\'ve developed a new aim-envelope system.\n\n\n"
