      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Phase 2: Combat Engine & Enemy AI Foundation\n\nYou are continuing development of STARFALL.\n\nPhase 1 has been completed successfully.\n\nThe current flight prototype feels very good. Preserve its existing flight behavior and visual identity.\n\nYour task is to implement Phase 2: a complete foundational combat system.\n\nThis phase must integrate naturally with the existing flight prototype. Do not replace working systems unnecessarily.\n\n# 1. Existing Project Preservation\n\nBefore implementation:\n\nUse your context\nIdentify the correct integration points for combat.\nPreserve all existing working functionality.\n\nDo not rebuild the project from scratch.\n\nDo not replace the existing flight controller with a different implementation.\n\n# 2. Mouse Aiming Range Improvement\n\nThe current mouse flight controls feel good. However, the available aiming range is too small.\n\nImprove the effective mouse aiming range.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Inspect the existing mouse input mapping and aiming boundaries.\n- Identify why the usable aiming range feels restricted.\n- Expand the available aiming envelope so the player can aim across a significantly larger area.\n- Allow more extensive yaw and pitch control through mouse movement.\n- Preserve the current responsiveness and smoothness.\n- Keep small mouse movements precise.\n- Avoid simply increasing global mouse sensitivity.\n- Avoid introducing artificial restrictions that prevent the player from using the expanded range.\n- Preserve existing Pointer Lock behavior if it is already implemented correctly.\n- Keep the chase camera and ship orientation consistent with the expanded aiming range.\n- Ensure the ship does not behave unpredictably at extreme pitch or yaw values.\n\nThe result should feel like a more spacious and natural aiming system.\n\nDo not change acceleration, boost, roll, or other flight physics unless strictly required.\n\n# 3. Combat Architecture\n\nCreate a modular combat system.\n\nSeparate the following responsibilities:\n\n- Weapon configuration.\n- Weapon firing.\n- Projectile simulation.\n- Hit detection.\n- Damage calculation.\n- Health management.\n- Destruction handling.\n- Combat feedback.\n\nKeep combat logic independent from React UI components.\n\nUse TypeScript types and reusable configuration objects.\n\nUse the existing game loop and delta-time system.\n\nAvoid unnecessary per-frame allocations.\n\n# 4. Primary Weapon System\n\nImplement the player\'s primary weapon.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Left Mouse Button fires the primary weapon.\n- Support continuous firing while the button is held.\n- Implement configurable fire rate.\n- Implement projectile speed.\n- Implement weapon damage.\n- Implement projectile lifetime.\n- Implement firing cooldown.\n- Prevent invalid or excessively frequent firing.\n- Add muzzle flash and weapon firing effects.\n- Add appropriate laser or energy projectile visuals.\n- Add firing audio through the existing or newly organized audio system.\n\nWeapon parameters must be configurable.\n\nDo not hardcode weapon behavior throughout multiple unrelated files.\n\n# 5. Projectile System\n\nImplement an efficient projectile system.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Projectiles travel through 3D space.\n- Projectiles use delta-time-based movement.\n- Projectiles have a defined lifetime.\n- Projectiles disappear or return to an object pool when expired.\n- Projectiles can hit valid targets.\n- Projectiles must not damage their own shooter.\n- Projectiles must support future multiplayer synchronization.\n- Avoid creating and destroying large numbers of Three.js objects every frame.\n\nUse object pooling where appropriate.\n\nMake projectile visuals consistent with the STARFALL cinematic sci-fi style.\n\n# 6. Targeting and Aiming Feedback\n\nImprove combat readability.\n\nImplement:\n\n- A clear aiming reticle.\n- A visible firing direction indicator.\n- A target indicator when an enemy is detected.\n- Appropriate hit feedback.\n- Clear visual feedback when the weapon is firing.\n\nThe reticle must match the actual weapon firing direction.\n\nDo not display misleading targeting information.\n\nKeep the HUD minimal and professional.\n\nAvoid turning the HUD into a generic dashboard.\n\n# 7. Health and Damage System\n\nImplement a reusable health system for player ships and enemy ships.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Configurable maximum health.\n- Current health tracking.\n- Damage application.\n- Damage validation.\n- Destruction state.\n- Health display in the HUD.\n- Enemy health indicators when appropriate.\n\nAdd a clear damage response.\n\nWhen the player receives damage:\n\n- Show appropriate HUD feedback.\n- Play a suitable sound.\n- Provide a subtle visual indication.\n\nWhen an enemy is destroyed:\n\n- Trigger a destruction effect.\n- Remove it from active combat.\n- Update the combat statistics.\n\nPrevent destroyed ships from receiving additional damage.\n\n# 8. Collision Detection\n\nImplement reliable collision detection for:\n\n- Player projectiles.\n- Enemy ships.\n- Player ship.\n- Enemy projectiles.\n- Other relevant combat objects.\n\nUse suitable collision volumes.\n\nPrioritize performance and predictable behavior.\n\nAvoid expensive collision checks between every possible object when a more efficient approach is available.\n\nEnsure projectiles cannot repeatedly apply damage to the same target.\n\n# 9. Enemy Bot AI Foundation\n\nImplement the first functional enemy combat AI.\n\nThe initial AI should support:\n\n- Detecting the player.\n- Acquiring the player as a target.\n- Turning toward the player.\n- Pursuing the player.\n- Maintaining a suitable engagement distance.\n- Firing weapons.\n- Avoiding obviously ineffective movement.\n- Reacting to damage.\n- Handling destruction.\n\nUse a clear state-based AI architecture.\n\nSuggested states:\n\n- Patrol\n- Acquire Target\n- Pursue\n- Engage\n- Evade\n- Reposition\n- Destroyed\n\nImplement reasonable transitions between states.\n\nEnemy movement should use the existing flight simulation principles where practical.\n\nDo not create an enemy that simply teleports or rotates instantly toward the player.\n\nThe enemy should feel like a spaceship controlled by an AI pilot.\n\n# 10. Bot Personality Foundation\n\nPrepare four configurable bot personalities:\n\n1. Aggressor\n   - Frequently pursues the player.\n   - Uses offensive opportunities.\n   - Maintains pressure.\n\n2. Flanker\n   - Attempts to approach from different directions.\n   - Changes its approach angle.\n   - Avoids following directly behind the player all the time.\n\n3. Defender\n   - Maintains distance.\n   - Uses defensive movement.\n   - Attacks when opportunities appear.\n\n4. Ace\n   - Uses stronger pursuit and evasion decisions.\n   - Reacts more effectively.\n   - Uses more advanced movement patterns.\n\nFor this phase, implement the architecture and at least one fully functional personality.\n\nMake the other personalities configurable and ready for later refinement.\n\nDifficulty must eventually affect reaction time, accuracy, movement, firing frequency, and tactical decisions.\n\nDo not implement difficulty by simply giving enemies unlimited health or damage.\n\n# 11. Combat HUD\n\nIntegrate combat information into the existing HUD.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Player health.\n- Weapon status.\n- Firing state.\n- Target information when available.\n- Enemy health when appropriate.\n- Basic combat statistics.\n\nKeep the existing STARFALL visual style:\n\n- Deep navy.\n- Electric cyan.\n- Blue highlights.\n- Dark transparent panels.\n- Subtle futuristic details.\n\nPreserve the game view as the main visual focus.\n\n# 12. Destruction and Visual Effects\n\nImplement suitable effects for:\n\n- Weapon firing.\n- Projectile impacts.\n- Ship damage.\n- Ship destruction.\n\nUse high-quality visual effects while respecting performance.\n\nConsider:\n\n- Particle effects.\n- Emissive materials.\n- Short-lived light effects.\n- Explosion shaders.\n- Camera feedback.\n\nUse object pooling or reusable effects where appropriate.\n\nAvoid excessive dynamic lights and unnecessary post-processing.\n\n# 13. Audio\n\nImplement foundational combat audio:\n\n- Primary weapon firing.\n- Projectile impact.\n- Player damage.\n- Enemy destruction.\n\nUse Web Audio API or the project\'s existing audio architecture.\n\nAvoid introducing paid audio services.\n\nAudio must be configurable and should not create duplicate playback loops.\n\n# 14. Performance Requirements\n\nMaintain the project\'s performance standards.\n\n- Use delta-time-based simulation.\n- Avoid unnecessary React re-renders.\n- Avoid excessive object allocations in frame loops.\n- Use object pooling for projectiles and frequent effects.\n- Use efficient collision detection.\n- Dispose of resources safely.\n- Keep the Asset Manager responsible for loading and caching assets.\n- Do not load unrelated mission assets.\n- Maintain smooth rendering with multiple active combat objects.\n\nMeasure performance in the actual browser.\n\nDo not claim a 60 FPS result without testing it.\n\n# 15. Architecture and Future Multiplayer Compatibility\n\nPrepare combat systems for the planned host-based multiplayer architecture.\n\nKeep important game state separate from visual presentation.\n\nUse clear interfaces for:\n\n- Player identity.\n- Damage events.\n- Weapon firing events.\n- Projectile ownership.\n- Health updates.\n- Destruction events.\n- Combat statistics.\n\nDo not implement multiplayer networking in this phase.\n\nHowever, avoid designs that require a complete combat-system rewrite when multiplayer is introduced.\n\n# 16. Testing and Acceptance Criteria\n\nAfter implementation, test the actual game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Existing flight controls still work.\n2. Mouse aiming range is noticeably larger.\n3. Small mouse movements remain precise.\n4. The aiming reticle matches weapon direction.\n5. Primary weapon fires correctly.\n6. Continuous firing respects its configured fire rate.\n7. Projectiles move correctly.\n8. Projectiles hit valid enemies.\n9. The player\'s own projectiles do not damage the player.\n10. Health decreases correctly.\n11. Destroyed enemies cannot receive further damage.\n12. Enemy AI can acquire, pursue, and attack the player.\n13. Combat effects work correctly.\n14. Combat audio works correctly.\n15. Multiple projectiles and effects do not cause major performance problems.\n16. Existing menus and settings remain functional.\n\nFix issues discovered during testing.\n\n# 17. Scope Control\n\nThis is Phase 2.\n\nDo not implement:\n\n- The complete five-mission campaign.\n- Final ranking and unlock systems.\n- Multiplayer networking.\n- LAN room hosting.\n- Invitation-code infrastructure.\n- Full spaceship hangar progression.\n- Large-scale environment redesign.\n\nThose belong to later phases.\n\nBuild a complete and reliable combat foundation first.\n\n# 18. Final Report\n\nWhen Phase 2 is complete, provide:\n\n- Architecture changes.\n- New and modified files.\n- Implemented combat features.\n- Bot AI capabilities.\n- Mouse aiming improvements.\n- Performance measurements.\n- Tests performed.\n- Known limitations.\n- Recommended next phase.\n\nDo not claim tests passed unless they were actually executed.\n\nThe final result must be a playable combat prototype integrated into the existing STARFALL game."
    }
    captured_user_requests {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Phase 3: Mission Campaign, Progressive Loading & Performance Engineering\n\nYou are continuing development of STARFALL.\n\nPhase 1 established the 3D flight prototype.\n\nPhase 2 established the combat foundation.\n\nYour task is to build Phase 3: a complete single-player mission campaign, fix the remaining mouse movement limitation, and significantly improve asset loading and runtime performance.\n\nThis is an integration phase. Preserve existing working systems.\n\nDo not rebuild the game from scratch.\n\n# 1. Inspect the Existing Project\n\nBefore implementation:\n\n- Inspect the entire existing project structure.\n- Review the flight controller and mouse input implementation.\n- Review the combat and projectile systems.\n- Review enemy AI.\n- Review the existing game state architecture.\n- Review the Asset Manager and resource loading implementation.\n- Review rendering performance and existing optimization techniques.\n- Identify incomplete systems and integration issues.\n\nReuse existing implementations wherever practical.\n\nDo not create duplicate systems.\n\n# 2. Critical Mouse Movement and Turning Fix\n\nThere is still an important mouse-control issue.\n\nWhen the mouse reaches a screen boundary, it appears to automatically move or snap back. This interrupts the intended continuous turning behavior.\n\nThe desired behavior is continuous mouse-controlled ship rotation and camera movement without screen-edge restrictions.\n\nInvestigate the actual implementation.\n\nPossible causes include:\n\n- Manual cursor recentering.\n- Absolute cursor-coordinate calculations.\n- Incorrect Pointer Lock handling.\n- Input clamping.\n- Canvas boundary restrictions.\n- Incorrect mouse event handling.\n\nImplement a reliable solution.\n\nRequirements:\n\n1. Use the Pointer Lock API during active gameplay.\n2. Use relative mouse movement through movementX and movementY.\n3. Do not depend on absolute cursor coordinates for continuous turning.\n4. Remove unnecessary cursor recentering logic.\n5. Remove artificial screen-boundary restrictions.\n6. Do not clamp mouse input to a small central region.\n7. Allow continuous yaw and pitch input regardless of the physical screen boundaries.\n8. Preserve existing mouse sensitivity as closely as possible.\n9. Preserve precise small movements.\n10. Preserve smooth turning and camera response.\n11. Handle pointer-lock activation and release correctly.\n12. Preserve ESC pause behavior.\n13. Restore mouse control correctly when gameplay resumes.\n\nImportant clarification:\n\nPointer Lock normally hides the operating-system cursor while allowing continuous relative mouse movement.\n\nDo not attempt to simulate unlimited absolute cursor coordinates.\n\nIf a visible aiming indicator is needed, render a separate in-game virtual reticle.\n\nThe desired result is uninterrupted continuous turning, not a cursor that repeatedly snaps back to the center.\n\nDo not change flight acceleration, boost, roll, or unrelated camera behavior.\n\n# 3. Mission Campaign Architecture\n\nCreate a reusable mission management system.\n\nEach mission should have its own configuration.\n\nSupport:\n\n- Mission identification.\n- Mission name.\n- Description.\n- Difficulty.\n- Enemy composition.\n- Enemy behavior configuration.\n- Mission objectives.\n- Completion conditions.\n- Failure conditions.\n- Mission statistics.\n- Rewards.\n- Unlock requirements.\n\nKeep mission configuration separate from mission execution logic.\n\nAvoid hardcoding each mission as an unrelated implementation.\n\n# 4. Five Initial Missions\n\nImplement these five missions.\n\n## Mission 01 \342\200\224 First Contact\n\nDifficulty: Rookie\n\n- Two enemy bots.\n- Introduce basic combat.\n- Teach targeting and weapon usage.\n- Objective: eliminate all enemies.\n\n## Mission 02 \342\200\224 Hostile Squadron\n\nDifficulty: Normal\n\n- Four enemy bots.\n- Introduce coordinated attacks.\n- Mix offensive and defensive enemy behavior.\n- Objective: eliminate the hostile squadron.\n\n## Mission 03 \342\200\224 Ambush\n\nDifficulty: Veteran\n\n- Five enemy bots.\n- Enemies begin in different positions.\n- Encourage awareness and maneuvering.\n- Objective: survive and eliminate all hostiles.\n\n## Mission 04 \342\200\224 Ace Hunters\n\nDifficulty: Expert\n\n- Three elite enemy bots.\n- More advanced movement.\n- Better aiming and evasion.\n- Objective: defeat all elite pilots.\n\n## Mission 05 \342\200\224 Final Stand\n\nDifficulty: Elite\n\n- Multiple enemy waves.\n- Increasing pressure.\n- A final Ace opponent.\n- Objective: survive all waves and defeat the final enemy.\n\nUse the existing combat and AI architecture.\n\nDo not create a separate combat implementation for missions.\n\n# 5. Mission Objective System\n\nImplement a reusable objective system.\n\nSupport:\n\n- Eliminate all enemies.\n- Survive for a defined duration.\n- Defeat a specific target.\n- Survive multiple enemy waves.\n\nEach mission must track its objective progress.\n\nDisplay current objectives clearly in the HUD.\n\nProvide immediate feedback when an objective is completed or failed.\n\nMission completion must be determined by explicit game-state rules.\n\nAvoid relying on arbitrary timers when an objective does not require them.\n\n# 6. Difficulty System\n\nImplement configurable difficulty settings.\n\nDifficulty should affect:\n\n- Enemy reaction time.\n- Enemy aiming accuracy.\n- Enemy movement.\n- Firing frequency.\n- Evasion decisions.\n- Tactical behavior.\n- Enemy coordination.\n\nDo not implement difficulty by simply multiplying enemy health or damage without justification.\n\nKeep difficulty values configurable.\n\nMake sure each difficulty level produces a meaningful gameplay difference.\n\n# 7. Rank and Performance Scoring\n\nImplement four ranks:\n\n- S\n- A\n- B\n- C\n\nEvaluate mission performance using:\n\n- Combat performance.\n- Accuracy.\n- Survival.\n- Mission completion time.\n\nCreate a deterministic scoring system.\n\nUse clear scoring thresholds.\n\nThe same performance should always produce the same result.\n\nDisplay:\n\n- Final rank.\n- Enemies defeated.\n- Accuracy.\n- Time taken.\n- Damage received.\n- Mission completion status.\n\nDo not let an AI model randomly determine player ranks.\n\n# 8. Mission Progression and Unlocks\n\nImplement mission progression.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Mission 01 is initially available.\n- Completing a mission unlocks the next mission when its requirements are met.\n- Store completed mission results.\n- Preserve the player\'s best rank.\n- Preserve the best score.\n- Show locked and unlocked missions.\n- Display mission completion status.\n\nUse LocalStorage for initial local persistence.\n\nHandle invalid or outdated saved data safely.\n\nDo not introduce accounts or a backend.\n\n# 9. Mission Selection UI\n\nBuild a polished Mission Select screen.\n\nShow:\n\n- Mission number.\n- Mission name.\n- Difficulty.\n- Mission description.\n- Enemy count or wave information.\n- Lock status.\n- Completion status.\n- Best rank.\n\nUse the existing STARFALL visual identity.\n\nKeep the 3D game visual experience central.\n\nAvoid generic dashboard layouts.\n\nProvide clear navigation back to the main menu.\n\n# 10. Mission Loading and Loading Screens\n\nImplement a proper mission-loading experience.\n\nWhen a player selects a mission:\n\n1. Identify the required resources.\n2. Load the selected player\'s spaceship.\n3. Load the current mission environment.\n4. Load required enemy assets.\n5. Load required weapons and effects.\n6. Load relevant audio.\n7. Initialize the mission.\n8. Enter gameplay only when required resources are ready.\n\nDisplay loading progress.\n\nProvide useful loading states instead of a frozen screen.\n\nSupport cancellation or safe recovery from failed asset loading where practical.\n\nDo not load all mission assets during initial application startup.\n\n# 11. Progressive Asset Loading Architecture\n\nImprove the existing Asset Manager or build a centralized one if necessary.\n\nIt must support:\n\n- Lazy loading.\n- Dynamic imports.\n- On-demand model loading.\n- Mission-specific asset loading.\n- Ship-specific asset loading.\n- Texture loading.\n- Audio loading.\n- Background preloading.\n- Asset caching.\n- Reference tracking.\n- Safe resource disposal.\n- Loading progress.\n- Loading failure handling.\n\nUse clear asset identifiers and typed resource definitions.\n\nAvoid duplicate downloads and duplicate GPU resource creation.\n\nKeep shared resources alive while they are still referenced.\n\nRelease mission-specific resources when they are no longer needed.\n\nDo not dispose of resources that are still used by another scene or component.\n\n# 12. Resource Loading Strategy\n\nUse the following loading stages.\n\n## Stage A \342\200\224 Application Boot\n\nLoad:\n\n- Essential application code.\n- Main menu.\n- Basic UI assets.\n- Minimal background environment.\n- Asset Manager.\n\n## Stage B \342\200\224 Hangar\n\nLoad:\n\n- The currently selected spaceship.\n- Its materials and textures.\n- Its engine effects.\n- Relevant preview assets.\n\nLoad other ships only when needed or when safe background preloading is beneficial.\n\n## Stage C \342\200\224 Mission Preparation\n\nLoad:\n\n- Current mission environment.\n- Selected player ship.\n- Required enemies.\n- Weapon assets.\n- Relevant effects.\n- Mission audio.\n\n## Stage D \342\200\224 Gameplay\n\nLoad additional resources only when required.\n\nAvoid unnecessary large synchronous loading operations during combat.\n\n## Stage E \342\200\224 Mission Cleanup\n\nRelease unused mission resources.\n\nPreserve shared assets and cached resources when appropriate.\n\nDo not perform unsafe cleanup.\n\n# 13. Performance Engineering\n\nImprove performance without unnecessarily reducing visual quality.\n\nImplement or improve the following techniques where appropriate.\n\n## Rendering\n\n- Frustum culling.\n- Level of Detail.\n- Instanced rendering.\n- Efficient material reuse.\n- Efficient geometry reuse.\n- Controlled shadow casting.\n- Controlled dynamic lighting.\n\n## Simulation\n\n- Delta-time-based updates.\n- Avoid unnecessary allocations in frame loops.\n- Efficient collision detection.\n- Object pooling.\n- Avoid unnecessary React re-renders.\n\n## Effects\n\n- Pool projectiles.\n- Pool frequently reused particles.\n- Reuse explosion effects where practical.\n- Limit excessive dynamic lights.\n- Avoid unnecessarily expensive effects at long distances.\n\n## Asset Optimization\n\n- Texture compression where supported.\n- Appropriate texture resolutions.\n- Geometry optimization.\n- Model loading only when needed.\n- Safe GPU resource management.\n\nDo not blindly apply every optimization.\n\nInspect the actual project and use techniques that solve real bottlenecks.\n\n# 14. Adaptive Graphics Settings\n\nCreate or improve graphics presets:\n\n- Low.\n- Medium.\n- High.\n- Ultra.\n\nAllow appropriate settings for:\n\n- Shadow quality.\n- Particle quality.\n- Post-processing.\n- Environment detail.\n- Draw distance.\n- Resolution scaling.\n- Anti-aliasing where supported.\n\nEnsure settings actually affect rendering quality.\n\nDo not display settings that have no implementation.\n\nPreserve the visual identity across presets.\n\n# 15. Performance Monitoring\n\nAdd a development-only performance monitoring system.\n\nMeasure what can be reliably measured:\n\n- FPS.\n- Frame time.\n- Draw calls.\n- Triangle count.\n- Active projectiles.\n- Active particles.\n- Active enemies.\n- Asset loading time.\n- Resource counts where measurable.\n\nDo not expose unnecessary debugging information to normal players.\n\nUse measurements to identify actual bottlenecks.\n\nTarget approximately 60 FPS on modern desktop hardware.\n\nReport actual measurements and testing conditions.\n\nNever claim a performance target was achieved without testing it.\n\n# 16. React and Rendering Performance\n\nMaintain strict separation between UI updates and real-time simulation.\n\nDo not cause React to re-render the entire application every frame.\n\nUse Three.js objects, refs, and the existing frame update architecture for continuous simulation.\n\nUse React state for appropriate UI changes such as:\n\n- Health display.\n- Mission objective progress.\n- Menu navigation.\n- Loading progress.\n- Mission results.\n- Settings.\n\nAvoid unnecessary state updates.\n\n# 17. Future Multiplayer Compatibility\n\nPreserve the planned host-based multiplayer architecture.\n\nDo not implement networking in this phase.\n\nHowever:\n\n- Keep mission definitions independent of local rendering.\n- Keep combat events structured.\n- Keep player state separate from presentation.\n- Keep scoring rules deterministic.\n- Keep simulation logic reusable.\n- Avoid relying on browser-only UI state for authoritative gameplay decisions.\n\nFuture multiplayer should be able to reuse mission and combat rules.\n\n# 18. Testing\n\nTest the actual application.\n\nVerify:\n\nMouse:\n- Continuous movement works without screen-edge restrictions.\n- No unwanted cursor recentering interrupts turning.\n- Small movements remain precise.\n- Large movements produce predictable rotation.\n- ESC and pointer-lock behavior work correctly.\n\nMissions:\n- All five missions load.\n- Enemy counts are correct.\n- Mission objectives work.\n- Completion and failure conditions work.\n- Difficulty levels are meaningfully different.\n- Results are calculated correctly.\n- Rank scoring is deterministic.\n- Mission unlocks work.\n- Progress survives a browser refresh.\n\nLoading:\n- Initial startup does not load every asset.\n- Mission assets load when needed.\n- Loading progress is displayed.\n- Failed asset loading is handled safely.\n- Shared assets are not disposed prematurely.\n- Mission-specific resources are cleaned up safely.\n\nPerformance:\n- Test with multiple active enemies.\n- Test with multiple projectiles and effects.\n- Inspect FPS and frame time.\n- Inspect draw calls and triangle counts where available.\n- Check for major memory growth.\n- Verify graphics settings work.\n\nPreserve all previously working flight and combat functionality.\n\nFix regressions discovered during testing.\n\n# 19. Scope Control\n\nThis is Phase 3.\n\nDo not implement:\n\n- The full three-ship progression and unlock economy.\n- LAN multiplayer.\n- Invitation-code infrastructure.\n- Online signaling.\n- WebRTC multiplayer.\n- Full multiplayer room management.\n\nThose belong to later phases.\n\nFocus on completing the single-player campaign and establishing reliable loading and performance foundations.\n\n# 20. Final Report\n\nWhen finished, report:\n\n1. Mission system architecture.\n2. Five missions implemented.\n3. Objective and progression systems.\n4. Rank calculation rules.\n5. Mouse movement root cause and exact fix.\n6. Asset Manager improvements.\n7. Progressive loading behavior.\n8. Optimization techniques implemented.\n9. Graphics settings implemented.\n10. Performance measurements.\n11. Tests performed.\n12. Remaining issues.\n13. Recommended next development phase.\n\nDo not claim a feature is complete unless it is implemented and tested.\n\nThe final result must be a playable STARFALL single-player campaign with reliable mouse controls, progressive asset loading, and measurable performance improvements."
    }
  }
}
