  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Critical Mouse Boundary Rollback Fix\n\nThe previous mouse-control adjustment did not fix the issue.\n\n## Confirmed Problem\n\nDuring gameplay, when the mouse reaches a screen boundary, it automatically snaps or rolls back.\n\nThis interrupts continuous mouse-controlled spaceship rotation and camera movement.\n\nThe desired behavior is continuous turning without physical screen-edge restrictions.\n\n## 1. Investigate the Actual Root Cause\n\nInspect the entire mouse input implementation.\n\nSearch for all occurrences of:\n\n- `movementX`\n- `movementY`\n- `clientX`\n- `clientY`\n- `screenX`\n- `screenY`\n- `requestPointerLock`\n- `exitPointerLock`\n- `pointerlockchange`\n- `pointerlockerror`\n- `setPointerCapture`\n- `releasePointerCapture`\n- Mouse position reset or recentering logic\n- Mouse coordinate clamping\n- Canvas mouse event handlers\n- Any custom cursor positioning code\n\nTrace the complete input flow from browser mouse events to spaceship rotation and camera updates.\n\nIdentify exactly what causes the rollback.\n\nDo not assume Pointer Lock is already working correctly.\n\n## 2. Implement Correct Continuous Mouse Input\n\nUse the browser Pointer Lock API for active gameplay.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Request pointer lock on an explicit user interaction.\n- Listen for `pointerlockchange`.\n- Verify that the intended game canvas or game container owns pointer lock.\n- Use `event.movementX` and `event.movementY`.\n- Do not use absolute cursor coordinates for continuous mouse turning.\n- Do not manually move the cursor back to the center.\n- Do not use artificial cursor boundaries.\n- Do not use mouse position clamping to restrict turning.\n- Do not combine pointer lock with pointer capture unnecessarily.\n- Prevent duplicate mouse event listeners.\n- Correctly remove event listeners when the game component unmounts.\n\nWhile pointer lock is active, browser mouse movement should provide relative movement even when the physical mouse reaches the edge of the display.\n\n## 3. Check for Conflicting Systems\n\nInspect whether another system is overriding the correct mouse input.\n\nIn particular, check:\n\n- React event handlers.\n- Three.js canvas handlers.\n- Camera controller.\n- Flight controller.\n- UI overlays.\n- Input manager.\n- Any third-party controls.\n- Any code that resets mouse coordinates every frame.\n\nThere must be one clear source of mouse movement input.\n\nAvoid multiple systems independently controlling the same mouse movement.\n\n## 4. Verify Browser Behavior\n\nAdd temporary development diagnostics if necessary.\n\nDisplay or log:\n\n- `document.pointerLockElement`\n- Pointer-lock state.\n- `movementX`\n- `movementY`\n- Mouse input received per frame.\n\nUse these diagnostics to determine whether the browser stops sending relative movement or whether the game code discards the input.\n\nRemove unnecessary diagnostic UI before completion.\n\n## 5. Preserve Existing Flight Feel\n\nDo not change:\n\n- Flight acceleration.\n- Maximum speed.\n- Boost.\n- Roll.\n- Existing mouse sensitivity.\n- Existing camera smoothing.\n- Combat.\n- Weapons.\n- Mission systems.\n\nOnly correct the mouse input path and any directly related camera integration issue.\n\n## 6. Acceptance Tests\n\nTest in the actual browser.\n\n1. Enter gameplay and activate pointer lock.\n2. Move the physical mouse repeatedly toward every screen edge.\n3. Confirm the ship continues turning smoothly.\n4. Confirm no cursor snapping or rollback interrupts turning.\n5. Confirm `movementX` and `movementY` continue to reach the game.\n6. Confirm small movements remain precise.\n7. Confirm large movements produce continuous turning.\n8. Press ESC and verify pointer lock is released.\n9. Resume gameplay and verify pointer lock works again.\n10. Verify keyboard flight controls remain unchanged.\n\nIf the issue remains, do not report success.\n\nExplain whether the problem is caused by browser pointer-lock behavior, event handling, or game-side input processing.\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n- Exact root cause.\n- Relevant code location.\n- Exact fix.\n- Pointer-lock verification.\n- Actual browser test results.\n\nDo not modify unrelated game systems."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Critical Mouse Boundary Rollback Fix\n\nThe previous mouse-control adjustment did not fix the issue.\n\n## Confirmed Problem\n\nDuring gameplay, when the mouse reaches a screen boundary, it automatically snaps or rolls back.\n\nThis interrupts continuous mouse-controlled spaceship rotation and camera movement.\n\nThe desired behavior is continuous turning without physical screen-edge restrictions.\n\n## 1. Investigate the Actual Root Cause\n\nInspect the entire mouse input implementation.\n\nSearch for all occurrences of:\n\n- `movementX`\n- `movementY`\n- `clientX`\n- `clientY`\n- `screenX`\n- `screenY`\n- `requestPointerLock`\n- `exitPointerLock`\n- `pointerlockchange`\n- `pointerlockerror`\n- `setPointerCapture`\n- `releasePointerCapture`\n- Mouse position reset or recentering logic\n- Mouse coordinate clamping\n- Canvas mouse event handlers\n- Any custom cursor positioning code\n\nTrace the complete input flow from browser mouse events to spaceship rotation and camera updates.\n\nIdentify exactly what causes the rollback.\n\nDo not assume Pointer Lock is already working correctly.\n\n## 2. Implement Correct Continuous Mouse Input\n\nUse the browser Pointer Lock API for active gameplay.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Request pointer lock on an explicit user interaction.\n- Listen for `pointerlockchange`.\n- Verify that the intended game canvas or game container owns pointer lock.\n- Use `event.movementX` and `event.movementY`.\n- Do not use absolute cursor coordinates for continuous mouse turning.\n- Do not manually move the cursor back to the center.\n- Do not use artificial cursor boundaries.\n- Do not use mouse position clamping to restrict turning.\n- Do not combine pointer lock with pointer capture unnecessarily.\n- Prevent duplicate mouse event listeners.\n- Correctly remove event listeners when the game component unmounts.\n\nWhile pointer lock is active, browser mouse movement should provide relative movement even when the physical mouse reaches the edge of the display.\n\n## 3. Check for Conflicting Systems\n\nInspect whether another system is overriding the correct mouse input.\n\nIn particular, check:\n\n- React event handlers.\n- Three.js canvas handlers.\n- Camera controller.\n- Flight controller.\n- UI overlays.\n- Input manager.\n- Any third-party controls.\n- Any code that resets mouse coordinates every frame.\n\nThere must be one clear source of mouse movement input.\n\nAvoid multiple systems independently controlling the same mouse movement.\n\n## 4. Verify Browser Behavior\n\nAdd temporary development diagnostics if necessary.\n\nDisplay or log:\n\n- `document.pointerLockElement`\n- Pointer-lock state.\n- `movementX`\n- `movementY`\n- Mouse input received per frame.\n\nUse these diagnostics to determine whether the browser stops sending relative movement or whether the game code discards the input.\n\nRemove unnecessary diagnostic UI before completion.\n\n## 5. Preserve Existing Flight Feel\n\nDo not change:\n\n- Flight acceleration.\n- Maximum speed.\n- Boost.\n- Roll.\n- Existing mouse sensitivity.\n- Existing camera smoothing.\n- Combat.\n- Weapons.\n- Mission systems.\n\nOnly correct the mouse input path and any directly related camera integration issue.\n\n## 6. Acceptance Tests\n\nTest in the actual browser.\n\n1. Enter gameplay and activate pointer lock.\n2. Move the physical mouse repeatedly toward every screen edge.\n3. Confirm the ship continues turning smoothly.\n4. Confirm no cursor snapping or rollback interrupts turning.\n5. Confirm `movementX` and `movementY` continue to reach the game.\n6. Confirm small movements remain precise.\n7. Confirm large movements produce continuous turning.\n8. Press ESC and verify pointer lock is released.\n9. Resume gameplay and verify pointer lock works again.\n10. Verify keyboard flight controls remain unchanged.\n\nIf the issue remains, do not report success.\n\nExplain whether the problem is caused by browser pointer-lock behavior, event handling, or game-side input processing.\n\n## 7. Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n- Exact root cause.\n- Relevant code location.\n- Exact fix.\n- Pointer-lock verification.\n- Actual browser test results.\n\nDo not modify unrelated game systems."
    }
  }
