    thinking: "**Tracing the Codebase**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into the code, like a detective on the case! I\'m meticulously tracing every line, focusing particularly on `InputManager.ts` lines 103-13. I\'m cross-referencing this with real browser behavior and iframe interactions. It\'s crucial to understand the execution flow from the code\'s perspective.\n\n\n**Analyzing Pointer Lock Flow**\n\nI\'ve shifted my focus to the interplay between `App.tsx`, `FlightCanvas.tsx`, and `FlightHUD.tsx`, particularly concerning pointer lock. The `handleStartMission` function in `App.tsx` calls `requestPointerLock()` through `inputRef`, and I\'m carefully examining the conditional logic in `InputManager.ts` (lines 111-125) to understand how the fallback works when the initial request fails. The `Promise.catch` block is now under the microscope.\n\n\n**Unraveling Pointer Lock Timing**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical timing issue. The `.catch()` callback executes in a microtask, *after* synchronous user gestures expire, or if `unadjustedMovement` fails, leading to silent `requestPointerLock()` failures. I\'m questioning the use of `{ unadjustedMovement: true }` given Chrome\'s support and known bugs in macOS, Linux, and iframes. Furthermore, I\'m now examining `handleMouseDown` in `InputManager.ts` (lines 240-253) alongside code in `FlightCanvas.tsx` (lines 104-108) and `FlightHUD.tsx` (lines 591-601).\n\n\n**Investigating Dual Handlers**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical issue! Two distinct `mousedown` handlers, triggered on a single click, are calling `requestPointerLock()`. One originates in `FlightCanvas.tsx`, and the other in `InputManager.ts`. Furthermore, I\'ve just observed that `FlightHUD.tsx`, alongside `App.tsx` and `InputManager.ts`, includes a `handlePointerLockChange` function, and it\'s essential to understand their interactions.\n\n\n**Connecting the Dots**\n\nI\'m now linking the symptom to the code. Specifically, the observed mouse \"snap\" seems related to the fact that `FlightHUD.tsx` has `pointer-events-none` *except* on the pause button. This, alongside `handlePointerLockChange` within the same file, might be the root of the problem. My hypothesis is that when pointer lock is lost, the \"Click to Engage...\" button on the HUD becomes active, and an unintended click happens, due to pointer-events becoming enabled.\n\n\n"
