    thinking: "**Analyzing Boundary Behavior**\n\nI\'m now meticulously examining the \"mouse snaps at screen boundaries\" issue, diving deep into every single detail we\'ve identified so far. The initial focus is on the most likely causes of that behavior, considering all possibilities. I\'m prioritizing the two most salient explanations.\n\n\n**Investigating Pointer Lock**\n\nI\'m now deeply investigating the pointer lock functionality within the input manager, specifically lines 103-138 of `InputManager.ts`. My key focus is on understanding why `movementX`/`movementY` might suddenly stop or snap back, especially at screen boundaries. I\'m focusing on the behavior of `{ unadjustedMovement: true }` in various scenarios, including Chrome within an iframe and on different operating systems, particularly macOS, Windows, and Linux. I\'m especially concerned about the implications of the `.catch()` block when `unadjustedMovement` fails.\n\n\n**Unraveling Pointer Lock Issue**\n\nI\'m now grappling with an intriguing anomaly! If the ship **is** turning, so `pointerLocked` was `true` at some point, and when the mouse reaches the screen edge, it snaps, that\'s incredibly odd! I\'m now asking why that would happen in *browser* pointer lock! I\'m trying to identify the four primary mechanisms that could cause this behavior.\n\n\n**Examining Potential Causes**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical suspect: the interaction between `handleMouseMove`, `handlePointerLockChange`, and the OS/browser pointer lock within the sandbox, iframe, and Chromium itself. The clue is the screen boundaries causing snaps or rollbacks. I\'m focusing on line 234 in `InputManager.ts` and considering what Chromium does when pointer lock is active, especially on Windows, macOS, Linux, remote desktops, cloud previews, VMs, and iframes. The potential Chromium bug, the one with `unadjustedMovement`, mouse warping, or high-DPI/zoom/iframe issues, is now under deep investigation.\n\n\n**Uncovering Edge Warp Effect**\n\nI\'ve had a breakthrough! It seems that when the viewport is a certain size, and the browser warps the cursor from the edge back towards the center (a Chromium bug?), the `e.movementX` is a negative value that *our* code doesn\'t filter out, which then applies an opposite rotation! This could be a snap back in the opposite direction. But I\'m now checking `CameraController.ts`, `FlightController.ts`, and `GameScene.tsx` as potential other causes, to be thorough.\n\n\n**Evaluating Code Interaction**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the interaction between `FlightController.ts` and `CameraController.ts`, specifically examining how these modules influence ship motion and camera behavior. I\'ve re-examined the code in `FlightController.ts` at the specified lines, but found that this module only controls visual bank, and is not the root of the issue. Now, I\'m shifting focus to `CameraController.ts`, lines 96-103.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Problem Origin**\n\nI\'m now investigating potential causes in `CombatManager.ts`, specifically lines 690-700, and how that interacts with the camera and reticle position. I\'m focusing on the effect of rapid rotations on reticle behavior, and I\'m asking why there\'s no dependency on the mouse hitting screen boundaries. I\'m analyzing the effects of the clamped reticle position.\n\n\n**Examining Mouse Event Handlers**\n\nI\'m now focusing on potential double calls to `requestPointerLock`, and I\'m deeply concerned that there may be conflicting `mousedown` handlers, which could be the source of the screen boundary issue. I\'m carefully inspecting lines in `FlightCanvas.tsx` and `InputManager.ts` that handle `mousedown` events. I\'m focusing on why a duplicate `requestPointerLock` call may cause the issue, specifically at the boundary, and considering scenarios where pointer lock might be released prematurely. I\'m rechecking the condition `document.pointerLockElement === target` in `InputManager.ts`.\n\n\n**Investigating Conflicting Calls**\n\nI\'m now investigating a crucial conflict: multiple calls to `requestPointerLock`, and I\'m very concerned! `FlightHUD.tsx` calls `requestPointerLock` via a button, and `App.tsx` calls it even earlier before the loading screen is fully mounted! I\'m now verifying these calls, including how `LoadingScreen.tsx` mounts and unmounts with `fixed inset-0 z-40`, and I\'m analyzing how and why this potentially premature call might be conflicting, especially since the Launch button in `MissionSelectView` unmounts immediately during the click. Finally, I\'m verifying the `document.pointerLockElement` check to see if that\'s a source of the boundary issue, and looking at line 116 of `InputManager.ts`, the `unadjustedMovement` feature.\n\n\n**Analyzing Pointer Lock Fallback**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical point. The rejection of the `unadjustedMovement` promise within `.catch()` triggers `requestPointerLock()` in a microtask, and this runs in Chromium. I\'m now exploring scenarios involving both Chromium and the `movementX` behavior on various platforms when hitting screen boundaries with `unadjustedMovement: true` (or without it), looking for specific bugs. I\'m also examining if the behavior is different when calling the method WITH `unadjustedMovement: true` vs without. I\'m focusing on why a fallback call may cause a boundary issue.\n\n\n"
