  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing Mouse Events**\n\nI\'m now fixated on the output from real Chromium! I\'m dissecting the `RES` data meticulously, comparing Playwright\'s `page.mouse.move` (CDP\'s `Input.dispatchMouseEvent`) with a physical mouse\'s behavior. I am focusing on the disparities in the CDP results vs the expected results from a real mouse.\n\n\n**Unraveling Pointer Lock Nuances**\n\nI\'ve discovered a critical insight into pointer lock behavior. Headless Chrome\'s implementation warps the cursor post each `mouseMoved` event, causing an automatic inverse movement. This explains the observed cancellation of `mx` values. Furthermore, I\'m now investigating the impact of pointer lock within iframes in real Chrome on macOS/Linux/Windows, and observing how `unadjustedMovement`\'s failure and `pointerlockerror` (`ple`) relate to this unexpected behavior. I am diving into the `PLAYING!` events to ascertain the root cause. My focus has shifted from Headless to understanding real Chrome edge cases as the potential cause.\n\n\n**Investigating Pointer Lock Failure**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the five mechanisms that cause mouse \"snapping\" at screen boundaries. I\'m especially focused on scenarios where Pointer Lock fails, as it does within iframes or due to `unadjustedMovement: true` and the `pointerlockerror` event. It seems the rejection of the Pointer Lock promise on macOS/Linux may be crucial, potentially revealing edge case bugs.\n\n\n**Examining Pointer Lock Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now deeply engrossed in understanding Pointer Lock\'s behavior, particularly when active in desktop Chrome on macOS, Windows, and within iframes. I\'m focusing on scenarios when the hidden cursor hits boundaries, as I\'m thinking about Chromium\'s `RenderWidgetHostView` and `PointerLockController` in real-world browsers. Specifically, I\'m examining the `movementX`/`movementY` glitches at viewport or screen edges within iframes and on macOS/Linux (XWayland/X11). I am also investigating the interplay of `requestPointerLock()` calls, even when `LoadingScreen` has finished.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Cursor Warp**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a critical Chromium quirk. When `unadjustedMovement` is off (like on macOS/Linux), the OS cursor warps back to the lock center upon hitting a window edge, and this causes Chromium to emit synthetic `mousemove` events with massive `movementX`/`movementY` values. I am investigating the implications of a 600px movement threshold being far too high for many scenarios, like iframes.\n\n\n**Uncovering Rotation Mechanics**\n\nI\'ve just had a major realization, the `-600` value is a red herring! It is **FALSE**. Instead, the `InputManager` immediately reverses a significant yaw, the amount being applied `-420px` or `-310px`. I am now mathematically examining the `FlightController` and `CameraController` classes for any unexpected snapping or rollback. Tracing lines in `FlightController.ts` reveals a pure local-space rotation sequence with no gimbal lock, therefore no sudden snaps. The rotation order confirms `YXZ` incremental motion.\n\n\n**Analyzing Camera Lag**\n\nI\'m now focused on camera dynamics, specifically lag behavior during continuous pitching and rolling. I\'m exploring the implications of `visualBankAngle` and `yawVelocity` interactions, especially if `movementX` ceases, causing camera rollback. I am currently examining `CameraController.ts`, especially lines 96-103, and the impact of the `upAlpha` lerp during pitch/roll loops. I think this might be a key.\n\n\n"
