    thinking: "**Pinpointing Root Causes**\n\nI\'ve just finished a complete sweep, a full inspection of every line of code related to mouse, pointer lock, camera controls, and aiming across the entire codebase. This comprehensive review gives us an excellent foundation to pinpoint the exact root causes. Now, I will meticulously assemble all the evidence to address these issues systematically!\n\n### Root Cause 2: Broken Too\n\n\n**Analyzing Lock Sequencing**\n\nI\'ve been drilling down on the pointer lock request sequence. It appears the initial attempt, the one for unadjusted movement, can reject asynchronously and trigger a `pointerlockerror` event if that option isn\'t supported. This causes the system to emit a `pointerLocked = false` signal prematurely, and also the fallback can fail transient activation rules! The current fix is to bypass the unsupported first request.\n\n\n**Refining Gesture Handling**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the interaction of user gestures and pointer lock. My findings show removing `{ unadjustedMovement: true }` is the key to preventing the `NotSupportedError` and `pointerlockerror` issues. Using `target.requestPointerLock()` directly, without that option, avoids these problems entirely. It succeeds immediately and synchronously.\n\nI\'ve also observed the Chromium\'s pointer-lock behavior at screen boundaries. The `movementX` and `movementY` values spike due to cursor warping.\n\n\n**Investigating Warp Behavior**\n\nI\'m now digging into the cursor warping at viewport boundaries, and how Chromium handles it. The OS emits synthetic mousemove jumps (center minus edge) on the warp frame. My key insight is that our current threshold of 600px in `InputManager.ts` is completely inadequate for normal viewports, as boundary warp events are getting directly processed! This leads to a jarring camera snap, as the large warp spike rotates the ship backward drastically.\n\n\n**Detecting Warp Spikes**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on a precise detection strategy for cursor warp spikes. My plan is to differentiate between genuine, rapid mouse flicks and those artifacts from the OS / Chromium. We have to detect the synthetic jumps in `movementX` and `movementY` due to boundary warp. We need to measure the magnitude of the displacement to filter out the warps. My thought is that we should filter out huge single-event jumps, while measuring the rate and direction of adjacent mousemove events. I\'m focusing on detecting opposing direction events that can cancel / roll back!\n\n\n**Defining Warp Signatures**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining the precise signature of cursor-warp bugs, both in Chromium and other OS environments. I\'ve identified three key scenarios: an immediate bounce (opposite direction within 40ms or large jump in the opposite direction from prior movement) and a jump that\'s half the viewport dimension or greater. The Chromium and OS warp signatures seem very similar. I\'m investigating Chromium\'s CDP behavior with headless instances, which first sends a large move, and then a reverse warp move! The difference is key for us.\n\n\n**Implementing Warp Filtering**\n\nI\'m now devising the warp filtering implementation. The core idea involves a dynamic viewport-aware guard: if the absolute raw movement exceeds a threshold (e.g., smaller than the viewport size\'s percentage), I\'ll ignore the event. I need to be careful not to discard legitimate fast mouse flicks. I also need to make the guard adaptive to varying viewport sizes.\n\n\n**Refining Warp Detection**\n\nI\'m now refining the warp detection threshold and logic. I\'ve been experimenting with a viewport-relative guard. I\'m exploring an absolute anomaly threshold and a directional rollback check. My goal is to prevent unwanted camera snaps, while avoiding false positives for legitimate mouse flicks. I\'m looking at combining a magnitude-based absolute filter with a direction-reversal check to accurately identify the warps.\n\n\n**Validating Jump Signatures**\n\nI\'ve confirmed my hypothesis: cursor warps manifest as singular, massive jumps in `mousemove` events, unlike natural hand movements. A quick jump from +14 to -280 in a single event is 100% a cursor-warp glitch. Human hands always decelerate. We have to filter the spurious jumps and events!\n\nI\'m also now looking at duplicate mouse handlers in FlightCanvas.tsx and InputManager.ts, and I\'m going to determine which ones are conflicting with our core gameplay loop.\n\n\n**Verifying Lock Ownership**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on verifying pointer lock ownership. I\'ve realized that `InputManager.ts` isn\'t checking if our game element actually owns the lock, using `document.pointerLockElement === target`. I\'ll also ensure `handleMouseMove` consistently verifies ownership, instead of caching this boolean value. I\'m investigating how conflicting listeners in `FlightCanvas.tsx` are causing problems with the core gameplay loop.\n\n\n**Refining Event Handling**\n\nI\'m now deeply immersed in the nuances of mouse event targeting. I\'ve realized clicking anywhere on the viewport (even on a transparent HUD) should be correctly handled. I\'ve found an elegant solution in `InputManager.handleMouseDown`. Also, I am now analyzing premature `requestPointerLock()` calls during the Launch sequence and if the `requestPointerLock()` call on the Launch button click works correctly and allows Pointer Lock to be active immediately when the mission finishes loading.\n\n\n\n**Clarifying Lock State**\n\nI\'m now focusing on edge cases regarding pointer lock during the loading sequence. Specifically, if the `LoadingScreen` is visible while the pointer is locked, the cursor is hidden, which breaks the UX. I have to think: Should we request pointer lock on \"Resume\" / on clicking the canvas or on \"Launch?\" According to Requirement 6, we should only lock pointer after entering gameplay. Also, it is problematic that the user can click \"Cancel\" on the loading screen, but they won\'t see their mouse due to pointer lock. This problem arises if `active` is `false`, and `pointerLocked` is `true`.\n\n\n**Verifying Lock Transitions**\n\nI\'m now assessing how pointer lock behaves during gameplay transitions. Specifically, I\'m verifying if `requestPointerLock()` on \"Launch,\" \"Resume,\" or canvas click causes browser issues when `LoadingScreen` is mounted. Chromium worked, except during \"Cancel.\" I need to check `document.pointerLockElement` in `handlePointerLockChange` and determine why `pointerlockerror` happened. It was the `unadjustedMovement` option!\n\n**Analyzing Mouse Delta**\n\nI\'m now investigating the impact of continuous mouse movement on the camera control calculations, to ensure smooth rotation. I\'m focusing on how `shapedDx` and `radPerPx` influence yaw and pitch calculations and how this affects the `stepYaw` values in each frame. Currently, my observations suggest a continuous and smooth rotation without gimbal lock or rollback, thanks to the quaternion multiplication. It appears the Euler angles in `FlightController.ts` are purely for HUD display.\n\n\n**Examining Camera Updates**\n\nI\'m now closely reviewing `CameraController.ts`, specifically lines 96-113.  I\'ve confirmed the camera\'s up-vector tracking and smooth rotation logic. This system appears stable, with no issues that can be noticed.  The camera rotation utilizes `slerp` for smooth transitions.\n\n\n**Confirming Coordinate Independence**\n\nI\'m now confirming the 3D world space coordinate independence of `flight.position` and `flight.quaternion`, specifically related to camera smooth tracking. The system robustly handles 90\302\260 pitches, 180\302\260 rolls, and 360\302\260 loops using smooth lerping for up-vector, preventing gimbal lock. Moreover, it will only copy `_up` if it\'s very close.\n\n**Analyzing HangarView Interactions**\n\nI\'ve determined that `HangarView.tsx` poses no interference with gameplay. The pointer capture is exclusively limited to menu interaction and is unmounted during the gameplay phase. It\'s safe.\n\n**Analyzing Pointer Lock in AI Studio**\n\nI\'m examining how Chromium handles `document.pointerLockElement` and `mousemove` events when pointer lock is active versus inactive, especially when used in Google AI Studio. The focus is to understand potential differences in event behavior across these states and environments.\n\n\n**Investigating Event Behavior**\n\nI\'m now diving into the nuances of `mousemove` and `pointermove` events, particularly when pointer lock is active. It turns out Chromium fires both events with the same `movementX` and `movementY` values.  My focus now is on understanding how `requestPointerLock()` behaves with `{ unadjustedMovement: true }`. I\'m going to determine why it\'s rejected with `NotSupportedError` and triggers `pointerlockerror`.\n\n\n**Detecting Warp Events**\n\nI\'ve discovered Chromium generates `movementX` and `movementY` spikes back toward the center on cursor-warp. More critically, I observed `InputManager.ts` isn\'t using `isTargetPointerLocked()` to verify pointer lock ownership, and `handleMouseMove` fails to consistently verify ownership either. It currently just caches a boolean value.\n\n\n**Refining Lock Validation**\n\nI\'m now focusing on ensuring that we properly validate the pointer lock\'s state and ownership in several places. The key is to verify lock ownership using `document.pointerLockElement !== this.lockTarget` and to check for `pointerLockElement` consistently in `handleMouseMove`. My goal is to ensure `FlightCanvas.tsx` doesn\'t conflict with `InputManager.ts` to fix the issues.\n\n\n**Tracing Lock Acquisition**\n\nI\'m now tracing the exact event sequence when a user clicks to engage pointer lock, particularly focusing on the \"Click to Engage...\" button and the canvas interaction. I\'ve discovered a double request for pointer lock: a `mousedown` event on the button, then on the canvas `mouseup`, with the canvas click potentially triggering a second, problematic `requestPointerLock()` call. This leads to duplicate attempts, with the second request succeeding asynchronously, and `unadjustedMovement: true` may cause an error.\n\n\n**Verifying Lock State**\n\nI\'ve been meticulously tracing the sequence for pointer lock requests. The \"Click to Engage\" button followed by the canvas click initiates a double request. This results in the second request succeeding asynchronously. The `unadjustedMovement: true` option might also cause errors, even though `this.pointerLocked` still doesn\'t change until `pointerlockchange` fires. I\'ll test all aspects of `InputManager` with a headless Chromium test.\n\n\n**Revisiting Lock Logic**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on verifying pointer lock and event listeners, as per Section 2 of the requirements. I need to ensure we\'re listening for `pointerlockchange`, verifying lock ownership, preventing duplicate event listeners, and removing them correctly. I\'m taking a fresh look at `InputManager`, to guarantee alignment with these principles!\n\n\n**Refining Listener Management**\n\nI\'m now implementing safeguards to prevent duplicate listeners. I\'m ensuring `InputManager` is the sole source of mouse and pointer lock event handling, removing the `FlightCanvas` listener to eliminate conflicts. This will streamline the event handling in the app and minimize potential issues. I am updating to safeguard double `attach()` calls. I am now updating to check in `InputManager` if the click targets the canvas element for the pointer lock.\n\n\n**Refining Lock Acquisition**\n\nI\'m now investigating pointer lock acquisition, specifically the initial \"Click to Engage\" button and canvas interaction. It appears a double lock request happens: `mousedown` on the button, and `mouseup` on the canvas, potentially causing duplicate calls. Also, the current `requestPointerLock()` call, with `{ unadjustedMovement: true }`, fails and fires an error. I am going to try removing this option.\n\n\n**Eliminating Double Locks**\n\nI\'m now focused on eliminating the double pointer lock request triggered by the \"Click to Engage\" button and canvas click. Removing `unadjustedMovement: true` in the initial `requestPointerLock()` call resolved `NotSupportedError` and `pointerlockerror`. The current synchronous `target.requestPointerLock()` is more robust. I\'m also ensuring that `InputManager` is the single source for event handling.\n\n\n**Addressing Snap Anomalies**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the root causes of boundary snap/rollback, focusing on Chromium\'s cursor-warping behavior and synthetic mousemove events. I\'m actively working on detecting and filtering these \'warp\' spikes, which manifest as large, opposing-direction jumps in `movementX` and `movementY`. My priority is to develop a precise detection strategy to differentiate these artifacts from genuine mouse movements and eliminate jarring camera snaps.\n\n\n**Filtering Warp Jumps**\n\nI\'m now implementing the filter, designing it to distinguish Chromium\'s warping behavior from normal movements. I\'ll utilize an exponentially-decayed recent movement direction to detect the boundary rollback, a key step in preventing the jarring camera snaps. Now I\'ll conduct more testing!\n\n\n**Refining Warp Detection**\n\nI\'m now refining the warp detection implementation with a more robust filter, which uses exponentially decayed speed trackers. My plan now includes instantaneous glitch and warp-back reversal guards. I\'m focusing on dropping anomalous events to prevent the camera snaps, and I am also working on detecting opposing direction events that can cancel / roll back! I\'m now testing.\n\n\n**Implementing Warp Filtering**\n\nI\'ve been working on guards to filter boundary rollback events and eliminate jarring snaps. I\'ve designed the system to ignore jumps above the viewport percentage, and I\'m developing a test to refine the filters, making sure to avoid clamping legitimate fast mouse flicks.\n\n\n**Refining Warp Logic**\n\nI\'m now refining the warp detection logic and considering an edge case. The tests confirmed the effectiveness of the viewport-relative filter, blocking cursor warps. Now I need to ensure continuous turning without snapping. I am verifying that opposite direction events are correctly blocked, and I\'ll test how to deal with an initial \"warp back to center\" event.\n\nI am also examining how the cursor warps from the right edge, back to center. My tests show the system blocks those jumps, and permits seamless turning on the subsequent events. The camera will continue smoothly! I\'ve also checked the `App.tsx` file for pointer lock calls.\n\n\n**Refining Lock Activation**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the ideal time to activate pointer lock, considering the user experience and technical constraints. I\'ve realized that locking the canvas during the loading screen can trap the cursor, making it impossible to click \"Cancel.\" Based on the requirements, I believe the cleanest approach is to lock the pointer only upon entering gameplay, either when clicking \"Launch\" or \"Resume\", or with a canvas click after the assets have loaded. I\'ll make sure to get rid of all the bad edge cases for clicking cancel and launch.\n\n\n**Refining Lock Activation**\n\nI\'m now implementing the optimal pointer lock strategy. I will activate the lock on canvas click after launch, to prevent cursor traps. On real GPUs, the transition is seamless, as `LoadingScreen` is brief. I\'m verifying no React re-renders interfere with the gameplay loop, by checking `App.tsx`, `FlightCanvas.tsx`, and `GameScene.tsx` for listener issues.\n\n\n**Refining Render State**\n\nI\'m now diving into the continuous mounting of the canvas element. I am confirming this doesn\'t reset `WebGL` context or camera parameters. I\'ve re-reviewed `FlightCanvas.tsx` lines 60-77, and found it adds webgl event listeners, and then sets the lock target. I am examining lines 112 for graphics, and also `CameraController.ts` and `FlightController.ts` for any problems.\n\n\n**Revisiting Controller Analysis**\n\nI\'m revisiting the camera control to ensure continuous, snap-free rotation. I\'m carefully reviewing `CameraController.ts`, specifically lines 64\342\200\223113. I am confident that the current logic will prevent any snaps or rollbacks. I checked the line-by-line function, including up-vector calculations, sensitivity adjustments, and distance dynamics. I see no issues, and the camera follows and smoothly moves.\n\n\n**Verifying Continuous Rotation**\n\nI\'m now analyzing `CameraController.ts`, specifically lines 96-113, and I\'ve reviewed the up-vector tracking and smooth rotation logic. I\'m confirming no snap or roll back occurs, even with 360-degree continuous turning. The code utilizes `slerp` for smooth transitions. The system\'s up-vector, and smooth rotation logic, are stable.\n\n\n**Verifying Rate Normalization**\n\nI\'m now checking `FlightHUD.tsx`, `FlightController.ts`, and `CombatManager.ts`. Specifically, I\'m analyzing how `telemetry` updates yaw, pitch, and roll rates. These rates are correctly clamped between -1 and 1 using `THREE.MathUtils.clamp` within `FlightController.ts`. There are no anomalous values, and they\'re correctly normalized, leading to smooth rotation values!\n\n\n**Verifying Aim Clamping**\n\nI\'ve verified smooth rotation and normalized rates. Additionally, I\'ve checked `CombatManager.ts` lines 690-694, confirming aim target clamping and correct `telemetry` updates.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nI\'m now investigating a subtle possibility. I\'m verifying that even in sandboxed environments, `requestPointerLock` functions correctly. My tests will ensure the code still uses `event.movementX` and `event.movementY` while verifying that we have removed `{ unadjustedMovement: true }` so no errors trigger, and the filtering system prevents cursor warp spikes.\n\n\n**Confirming Lock Acquisition**\n\nI\'ve confirmed that pointer lock is requested correctly now, specifically on canvas clicks! I\'m verifying the `InputManager` correctly handles lock requests from the canvas. The duplicate handlers and unadjusted movement issues are gone! The system now consistently validates lock ownership.\n\n\n**Implementing Diagnostic Logging**\n\nI\'m now focusing on implementing dev diagnostics as per Requirement 4. I plan to add temporary telemetry fields within the browser verification tests to capture `document.pointerLockElement`, pointer-lock states, `movementX`, `movementY`, and per-frame mouse input. This system will pinpoint whether the browser or our code discards the input, leading to a thorough fix.\n\n\n**Implementing Diagnostic Checks**\n\nI\'m now focused on verifying pointer lock in a live Chromium environment, and I\'ll use console logging or Playwright to confirm key lock states, event values, and camera movement. My tests will ensure we\'re getting `movementX`/`Y` per frame, no rollback at screen edges, and clean pause/resume behavior via the `ESC` key and \"Resume Flight\" action. I\'m also preparing for the audit of `InputManager.ts`, `FlightCanvas.tsx`, `App.tsx`, and `FlightHUD.tsx`.\n\n\n**Refining Lock Verification**\n\nI\'m now prioritizing lock validation. My focus is on robustly handling the return of `document.pointerLockElement === this.lockTarget` and a consistent `requestPointerLock()` call. I\'m verifying that if the `pointerLockElement` isn\'t our target, we don\'t proceed. We want a clean, synchronous `requestPointerLock()` call.\n\n\n**Refining Warp Filtering**\n\nI\'ve been working on a robust `handleMouseMove` fix, carefully implementing ownership checks and warp spike filtering. I\'m prioritizing validation of pointer lock ownership, and filtering extreme movement anomalies and reversals, which now correctly address warp issues. Additionally, I\'m now integrating an exponentially decayed movement direction to detect boundary rollbacks.\n\n\n**Refining Warp Filtering**\n\nI\'ve implemented a filter to eliminate cursor-warp anomalies. The system rejects jumps exceeding thresholds and uses decay to detect boundary rollbacks. Also, a fix now clamps to raw deltas! I\'ll now be accumulating valid mouse deltas. I am now focused on a fix for handleMouseDown and detach, and I\'ll remove an old handler from FlightCanvas.tsx.\n\n\n**Refining Lock Activation**\n\nI\'m now completely satisfied with the single `InputManager` event listener. This guarantees pointer lock is consistently requested through a clean path. I\'m verifying the `onClick` event on the button is correctly handled and calls `inputRef.current.requestPointerLock()`. Also, `requestPointerLock()` now works synchronously and reliably! I am now focused on a smooth launch of lock activation.\n\n\n"
