  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Mouse Boundary Rollback Still NOT Fixed\n\nThe mouse boundary rollback issue is STILL NOT FIXED.\n\nThe previous two attempts did not solve the problem.\n\nWhen I move the physical mouse toward the edge of the screen, it still automatically snaps or rolls back to the middle. This interrupts continuous spaceship turning and camera movement.\n\nI do not want another speculative fix. I need the mouse to stay at the edge of the screen to make the ship keep turning when I want to.\n\n## Your Task: Find the Actual Root Cause\n\nStop making assumptions and stop applying random changes to mouse sensitivity, camera smoothing, or input boundaries.\n\nInvestigate the existing implementation from beginning to end.\n\n### 1. Trace the Entire Input Pipeline\n\nFollow the mouse input through every relevant component:\n\n- Browser mouse events.\n- Pointer Lock API.\n- Canvas and DOM event handlers.\n- Input manager.\n- Mouse coordinate processing.\n- Flight controller.\n- Camera controller.\n- Spaceship rotation.\n\nFind exactly where the rollback originates.\n\n### 2. Investigate Conflicting Implementations\n\nSearch the entire project for:\n\n- Cursor recentering.\n- Mouse coordinate resets.\n- `clientX` and `clientY` calculations.\n- `movementX` and `movementY` handling.\n- Input clamping.\n- Pointer Lock requests and releases.\n- Pointer capture.\n- Multiple mouse event listeners.\n- Camera code that overrides mouse rotation.\n- Any code that moves or constrains the cursor.\n\nCheck whether another input or camera system is overriding the intended behavior.\n\n### 3. Verify What Actually Happens\n\nUse browser diagnostics to establish:\n\n- Whether Pointer Lock is active.\n- Which element owns Pointer Lock.\n- Whether relative mouse movement events continue arriving.\n- Whether mouse input is discarded, reset, or overwritten.\n- Whether the rollback occurs in browser behavior or game code.\n\nDo not report a suspected cause as a confirmed cause.\n\n### 4. Fix Only After Identifying the Cause\n\nOnce the exact root cause is established:\n\n- Explain the cause.\n- Implement the appropriate correction.\n- Preserve the current flight feel.\n- Preserve mouse sensitivity.\n- Preserve camera behavior unless directly responsible.\n- Avoid unrelated modifications.\n\n### 5. Test Before Reporting Success\n\nTest the actual browser application.\n\nMove the physical mouse repeatedly toward all four screen edges.\n\nVerify that spaceship turning continues without snapping or rollback.\n\nIf the problem remains, report that it remains unresolved.\n\nDo not claim success based only on code inspection or successful compilation.\n\n## Required Final Report\n\nProvide:\n\n1. Why the previous two attempts failed.\n2. The actual root cause.\n3. The exact files and code responsible.\n4. What you changed.\n5. Evidence from browser testing.\n6. Whether the issue is genuinely fixed.\n\n**Important: The mouse boundary rollback has already survived two attempted fixes. Treat this as an unresolved debugging problem, not another routine input adjustment. Do not move on to other development tasks until the actual cause is identified.**"
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Mouse Boundary Rollback Still NOT Fixed\n\nThe mouse boundary rollback issue is STILL NOT FIXED.\n\nThe previous two attempts did not solve the problem.\n\nWhen I move the physical mouse toward the edge of the screen, it still automatically snaps or rolls back to the middle. This interrupts continuous spaceship turning and camera movement.\n\nI do not want another speculative fix. I need the mouse to stay at the edge of the screen to make the ship keep turning when I want to.\n\n## Your Task: Find the Actual Root Cause\n\nStop making assumptions and stop applying random changes to mouse sensitivity, camera smoothing, or input boundaries.\n\nInvestigate the existing implementation from beginning to end.\n\n### 1. Trace the Entire Input Pipeline\n\nFollow the mouse input through every relevant component:\n\n- Browser mouse events.\n- Pointer Lock API.\n- Canvas and DOM event handlers.\n- Input manager.\n- Mouse coordinate processing.\n- Flight controller.\n- Camera controller.\n- Spaceship rotation.\n\nFind exactly where the rollback originates.\n\n### 2. Investigate Conflicting Implementations\n\nSearch the entire project for:\n\n- Cursor recentering.\n- Mouse coordinate resets.\n- `clientX` and `clientY` calculations.\n- `movementX` and `movementY` handling.\n- Input clamping.\n- Pointer Lock requests and releases.\n- Pointer capture.\n- Multiple mouse event listeners.\n- Camera code that overrides mouse rotation.\n- Any code that moves or constrains the cursor.\n\nCheck whether another input or camera system is overriding the intended behavior.\n\n### 3. Verify What Actually Happens\n\nUse browser diagnostics to establish:\n\n- Whether Pointer Lock is active.\n- Which element owns Pointer Lock.\n- Whether relative mouse movement events continue arriving.\n- Whether mouse input is discarded, reset, or overwritten.\n- Whether the rollback occurs in browser behavior or game code.\n\nDo not report a suspected cause as a confirmed cause.\n\n### 4. Fix Only After Identifying the Cause\n\nOnce the exact root cause is established:\n\n- Explain the cause.\n- Implement the appropriate correction.\n- Preserve the current flight feel.\n- Preserve mouse sensitivity.\n- Preserve camera behavior unless directly responsible.\n- Avoid unrelated modifications.\n\n### 5. Test Before Reporting Success\n\nTest the actual browser application.\n\nMove the physical mouse repeatedly toward all four screen edges.\n\nVerify that spaceship turning continues without snapping or rollback.\n\nIf the problem remains, report that it remains unresolved.\n\nDo not claim success based only on code inspection or successful compilation.\n\n## Required Final Report\n\nProvide:\n\n1. Why the previous two attempts failed.\n2. The actual root cause.\n3. The exact files and code responsible.\n4. What you changed.\n5. Evidence from browser testing.\n6. Whether the issue is genuinely fixed.\n\n**Important: The mouse boundary rollback has already survived two attempted fixes. Treat this as an unresolved debugging problem, not another routine input adjustment. Do not move on to other development tasks until the actual cause is identified.**"
    }
  }
