  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the User Input**\n\nOkay, stop everything! The user is having a significant issue: their mouse input is getting hijacked. It seems their mouse cursor is snapping back to the center of the screen, which is completely messing up the continuous controls. This is a big deal and needs immediate attention before we go down any further paths.\n\n\n**Re-evaluating the Problem**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial discovery. The user isn\'t just dealing with basic mouse input issues; they are using an off-center reticle and the cursor snapping back is completely disrupting their flight controls. The user\'s goal is to have the mouse stay at the edge of the screen so their virtual reticle can stay at the edge of the screen, as well, for continuous turning. I need to focus on this virtual cursor and how it\'s being managed.\n\n\n**Tracing the Mouse Input**\n\nOkay, I\'m deep diving into the code now. I\'m focusing on the interaction between `FlightHUD.tsx`, `CombatManager.ts`, `FlightController.ts`, `CameraController.ts`, `InputManager.ts`, and `GameScene.tsx`. I\'m meticulously tracing the path of the mouse input, frame by frame, especially what happens when the user moves the mouse to the right and then stops. I\'m checking the calculations in `FlightController.ts` regarding `pendingYawRad` and how the quaternion is manipulated. I have to understand the shape of the function being applied to `movementX` so I can solve the issue.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Culprit**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the core of the problem. When the user stops moving the mouse, or reaches the edge of their input device, the yaw velocity decays to zero, causing the ship to stop turning. The camera then catches up, and the aim reticle ceases movement. This is a direct consequence of `mouseDeltaX` going to zero, and the subsequent calculations affecting `pendingYawRad` and `stepYaw`. I now understand the specific calculations that need adjustment.\n\n\n**Investigating the Reticle Behavior**\n\nI\'ve hit a major snag: the projected aim reticle (`aimReticleRef`) consistently snaps back to the screen\'s center (0.5, 0.5) automatically. I need to figure out why. I also need to verify what happens when pointer lock is not active or fails, and in an AI Studio preview iframe. We need to be careful to examine EVERY possibility thoroughly. I also need to go back and figure out how Phase 1 worked, and examine relevant flight telemetry fields. Let\'s make sure that our changes are correctly preserving the original behaviors.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Objective**\n\nAlright, I\'ve got it. The user has made it abundantly clear: they need their physical mouse to stay at the screen\'s edge to maintain continuous turning. This is NOT about speculative fixes or workarounds. I\'m focusing on why the cursor is snapping back to the center and how to prevent it. I\'m going to revisit `FlightController` and investigate how the reticle clamping and auto-centering mechanisms can be disabled so the player can aim across a significantly larger area. I\'ll meticulously compare this behavior to how it functioned in Phase 1.\n\n\n"
    message_id: "HhP_araxIKz4-sAPyZmQuA0"
