  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Verifying the Clues**\n\nI\'ve got it! It\'s all clear now! I\'m seeing how all the pieces fit together, like I\'m Holmes himself! Everything\'s lining up perfectly. I need to go over every single clue to double check my deduction, and I need to verify everything in the live code to confirm.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Events**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on two distinct issues! First, there\'s the snapping/rolling back when the mouse hits the screen\'s edge. This, as I understand it, breaks the continuous turning. Second, the user wants the mouse *to* remain at the edge. To get continuous turning. It seems these issues may both be present in `FlightController.ts` and require close inspection.\n\n\n**Examining the Visibility**\n\nI\'m now focused on the visible elements during gameplay, specifically the cyan crosshair, `aimReticleRef`, managed in `FlightHUD.tsx`. I\'ve examined `CombatManager.ts`, specifically lines 690-693. I am seeing how the clamped telemetry values, `aimScreenX` and `aimScreenY`, influence the visual position of the reticle based on the `_aimTarget` projection relative to the camera and the flight quaternion. It\'s key to understand how these values shift when a user stops moving their mouse, and how this relates to the edge-of-screen effect.\n\n\n**Investigating the Behavior**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the interaction with Pointer Lock. My understanding is that the system could be failing or releasing, contributing to the virtual joystick issues. I\'m focusing on two scenarios: when Pointer Lock is active, and when it isn\'t, and I\'m tracing what happens from the player\'s perspective in each case.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Cause**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the behavior when the user stops moving the mouse while expecting the ship to continue turning. The reticle snaps back to the center of the screen, and the ship stops turning. The `FlightController` seems to use FPS-style direct delta mode, so the ship only turns while the mouse is physically moving. When the mouse stops, the camera catches up, and the reticle rolls back to the center, ending the turning. I need to understand how to maintain the turn after the mouse stops moving.\n\n\n**Uncovering the Mechanism**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough! I examined `CameraController.ts` line 111, the `rotAlpha` value, and realized it\'s why the reticle snaps back to the center so fast! The camera quickly catches up to the ship\'s nose. This explains the user\'s observed snapping behavior, especially in light of the compaction summary. Phase 2 showed clamped `aimYawOffset` values that likely contributed to the issues.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Misconception**\n\nI\'ve traced the issue to a misunderstanding regarding Pointer Lock. Previously, there was a misinterpretation that led to changes that removed critical values like `aimYawOffset` in favor of a FPS-style approach, creating the very snapping behavior the user wants to avoid. Now, it\'s clear what they need and what they want to see, and I\'m correcting for the misinterpretation.\n\n\n**Investigating the Edge Cases**\n\nI\'m now diving into the implications of the application running within an `<iframe>` in Google AI Studio Preview. I am focusing on two scenarios; one where `allow-pointer-lock` is granted and another where it is not, to pinpoint the edge behavior. I need to understand how the browser, OS and iframe interaction affects Pointer Lock and the user\'s perception of the reticle. I must also check the case of Chrome on macOS and Windows to fully trace the flow.\n\n\n**Tracing the Scenarios**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the interaction with Pointer Lock, particularly within the context of the user\'s specific browser setup (macOS/Chrome). I\'m carefully analyzing `InputManager.ts` to see what happens when Pointer Lock is either active or inactive. The behavior when it is *inactive* is critical. I\'m focusing on why mouse movement is ignored completely and the implications for the user\'s continuous turning requirement. I will check for any unexpected behavior or variations based on OS/browser.\n\n\n"
