    thinking: "**Unveiling the Root Cause**\n\nI\'ve hit a major breakthrough; I\'ve nailed down the undeniable root cause, and it\'s far more extensive than initially conceived! I need to trace every single frame when playing this game in a real browser, to comprehend the entire pipeline. Let\'s trace all of the frames!\n\n\n**Tracing the Interaction Flow**\n\nI\'m now diving deep into how physical mouse movements translate to in-game actions. I\'ve focused on `InputManager`, `FlightController`, `CameraController`, `CombatManager`, and `FlightHUD`. I\'m tracing the events from the player clicking \"Launch\" to the aiming reticle\'s movement. Specifically, how positive mouse delta X values are handled by the `FlightController.update` function.\n\n\n**Analyzing the Behavior Shift**\n\nNow, I\'m examining the scenario when the player holds the mouse steady after an initial movement. It\'s clear that once `event.movementX` hits zero, `flight.yawVelocity` also becomes zero. The implications of this for the aiming reticle\'s behaviour are significant and I am starting to see how it can be exploited.\n\n\n**Clarifying the Core Issue**\n\nI\'ve just had a major epiphany! The exponential decay of `pendingYawRad` to zero is the culprit. `stepYaw` and the ship\'s rotation immediately halt, causing the `CameraController` to catch up and the reticle to snap back. This perfectly explains the user\'s struggle. The critical piece of info: the exponential decay means that the longer the mouse input is zero, the quicker the return. I\'m starting to see why the user\'s issue is arising from this core behavior. Now, I have to re-evaluate the four-turn history to fully comprehend the issue.\n\n\n**Uncovering the Design Flaw**\n\nI\'ve made a crucial discovery: the `FlightController` is using a virtual joystick, and I\'ve identified leftover telemetry fields hinting at this. I\'ve re-examined the user\'s initial mouse movements and how the reticle and ship reacted. My focus is now on the implemented offset, and it\'s clear the system is automatically recentering the reticle as the ship turns, which aligns with the user\'s report of a limited aiming range.\n\n\n**Pinpointing the Snapping Problem**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the snapping issue: it\'s not simply related to screen boundaries, but a fundamental flaw in the impulse-only FPS mouselook implementation. The previous agent erroneously removed the virtual cursor and replaced it with `pendingYawRad`, which causes an immediate rollback when the mouse stops moving. This is what\'s causing the reticle\'s snapping! Even the warp-spike filter in `InputManager` hasn\'t solved the issue. The ship\'s turning also halts when mouse movement stops. I\'m now fully analyzing this design defect to devise a more robust solution.\n\n\n**Confirming the Control Scheme**\n\nI\'ve just had a moment of clarity, realizing the user isn\'t asking for a speculative fix, but rather a core design change. The key phrase \"I need the mouse to stay at the edge of the screen to make the ship keep turning\" is a clear indicator of a virtual-joystick control scheme, common in space-fighter games. I\'m now fully verifying that every part of their reported behavior aligns with the requirements of this control scheme.\n\n\n**Validating the Concept**\n\nI\'m now completely validating the virtual joystick setup. The new focus is on how pointer lock inputs directly update `virtualAimX` and `virtualAimY`, ranging from -1 to 1. My intention is to ensure the steering cursor moves smoothly across the viewport, never automatically recentering or rolling back. Zero auto-recentering is the core of this design!\n\n\n**Refining the Virtual Joystick**\n\nI\'m now completely engrossed in how the virtual joystick will work. The virtual joystick maintains its position, and I\'ve focused on how `virtualAimX` and `virtualAimY` will behave in the new design. My main focus is on the behavior of continuous turning when a user puts the pointer off-center. I am also considering how this will affect the weapon aiming. I\'m focusing on the behavior of `virtualAimX` and `virtualAimY`, and the precise deadzone.\n\n\n**Defining the Visual Elements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the visual representation, specifically the distinct elements on the screen. There are two critical components: the ship\'s boresight indicator and the virtual flight cursor, which is 1:1 with the player\'s mouse movement. My focus is on ensuring this cursor stays at the screen edge when the mouse is there, never snapping or rolling back, and that there is a clean center reference when the mouse returns. I also need to consider the laser\'s firing location.\n\n\n"
