    thinking: "**Re-Evaluating Entire Scope**\n\nI\'ve hit the brakes and am zooming out. Now, I\'m meticulously scanning through a suite of files: `InputManager.ts`, `FlightController.ts`, `CameraController.ts`, `CombatManager.ts`, `FlightHUD.tsx`, and `App.tsx`. The goal? To guarantee, with absolute certainty, the absence of any insidious, lurking bugs.\n\n\n**Pinpointing Core Dynamics**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on the crux: `FlightController.ts`, specifically lines 222-252. The code dictates that the mouse movement translates to a transient velocity, decaying at 24Hz. This impulse-based system, with its exponential decay, is the root cause.\n\n\n**Analyzing Persistent State**\n\nI\'ve realized the core issue is the lack of a persistent steering cursor. The system doesn\'t hold the user\'s intended aim offset. `CombatManager.ts` projects the ship\'s nose as the aim target, which lags behind due to camera updates in `CameraController.ts`. The HUD renders the reticle at a position calculated from the ship\'s telemetry. Consequently, when the user stops moving the mouse, the reticle drifts back to the center as `pendingYawRad` decays.\n\n\n**Diagnosing Complete System Flaw**\n\nI\'ve traced the issue to `stepYaw` decaying to zero, causing the ship to stop turning, compounded by the camera catching up and the reticle snapping back to the screen center. The previous fixes failed due to misdiagnosis of a Pointer Lock problem and a failure to address the core problem of transient decay across multiple files. The persistent aim offset was deleted and not updated elsewhere, so the fixes failed. The second fix only touched `InputManager.ts` while leaving `FlightController.ts`, `CombatManager.ts`, and `FlightHUD.tsx` untouched, so the transient decay persists.\n\n\n**Investigating Mouse Movement**\n\nI\'m now laser-focused on `InputManager.ts` again. I\'m simulating different mouse movement scenarios within the pointer lock context. I\'m paying close attention to what happens when the physical mouse stops moving, specifically, what the `cursorX` value does. I\'m investigating how this relates to a persistent cursor, now.\n\n\n**Examining Edge Cases**\n\nI\'m now carefully examining the behavior of `cursorX` in `InputManager.ts` under Pointer Lock. I\'ve realized that if a user pushes the mouse past the edge, `cursorX` pins at +0.95 and doesn\'t wrap, maintaining continuous turning. Then, when the user moves the mouse back, the cursor smoothly returns, cleanly stopping the turn. I\'ve also just noticed a potential problem in how `isAnomalousWarpJump` handles fast flicks, which may cause a dropped input event.\n\n\n**Refining Event Filtering**\n\nI\'m now refining the event filtering logic. I\'ve realized I need to differentiate between extreme, impossible jumps, and legitimate fast flicks. I\'m focusing on the absolute value of raw movement. We should only guard against jumps that are mathematically impossible (`Math.abs(raw) > 500`), never normal human flicks.\n\n\n\n**Verifying Pointer Lock States**\n\nI\'m now investigating Pointer Lock states and examining `aimScreenX` values in both locked and unlocked scenarios. I need to understand what happens to the cursor in each case, especially regarding edge-of-screen behavior. My goal is to determine if differing Pointer Lock behaviors explain the observed issues. I\'m focusing on validating this in a headless Chromium environment with a diagnostic script.\n\n\n"
