  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Discovering New Insights**\n\nI\'ve hit a breakthrough! I just captured some incredible output within Chromium that\'s completely illuminating. This \"smoking gun\" data shows a clean move from moving_5 to CANVAS, with a reticle X position of 716 and a heading of 27. It\'s definitive, like a museum piece in the making.\n\n\n**Analyzing Edge-Hold Behavior**\n\nI\'m digging into these new data points, and the edge-hold behavior is crystal clear now. The reticle\'s X position snaps back to center (640) as soon as the edge is hit. The heading (hdg) freezes at 71 degrees the second the player hits the edge, as well.\n\n\n**Unraveling Core Mechanics**\n\nI\'ve got it! The pieces are falling into place, specifically with `aimYawOffset` in `FlightController.ts`. Before the attempts, it was pulling the cursor back to the center every frame. The first attempt scrapped the persistent cursor for a decaying `pendingYawRad`. This clearly shows that the underlying design is at the core of the problem, and my work is cut out for me in moving forward.\n\n\n**Designing Steering Solutions**\n\nI\'ve got a comprehensive grasp of what went wrong. The previous attempts were flawed. With the current logic, the camera lag forces a visual jump, returning the reticle to center, which feels bad. Also, with the reticle not persistent, the `pendingYawRad` decays to zero as the `CameraController` catches up. Both attempts failed to fix that. Now, I\'m setting about designing a perfect, AAA-grade virtual-cursor flight-steering system. It needs to feel incredible.\n\n\n**Crafting Input Refinements**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on an improved `InputManager.ts`. My key focus is a perfectly clean relative pointer lock input system, no matter the OS. I\'m focusing on cleaning up the pointer lock management and input listeners, especially around edge cases. I\'m fixing a flaw in filtering the absolute values to capture fast human mouse flicks. I will ensure no overly aggressive filters.\n\n\n**Refining Cursor Persistence**\n\nI\'m now completely clear on how to handle the virtual cursor. I need to make the cursor persistent during flight, never resetting or decaying it while the game is active. Resets should only happen on mission start or when pausing/unpausing, making sure to center the cursor at (0, 0) upon resuming to prevent unwanted ship movement. The goal is a clean, continuous turning experience inspired by Freelancer-style controls. I am now deep-diving into the exact math to make it perfect.\n\n\n**Implementing Cursor Logic**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the exact way `input.mouseDeltaX` and `input.mouseDeltaY` should update `aimCursorX` and `aimCursorY`. I\'ve created a clean, responsive sensitivity factor. It\'s scaled by `settings.mouseSensitivity` and the viewport scale, so moving the mouse a certain amount should reach the screen edge, no matter the frame rate. I\'m also implementing the y-axis inversion correctly. I\'m using `THREE.MathUtils.clamp` to make sure values stay within a range.\n\n\n**Verifying Inversion Logic**\n\nI\'m now deep into the `invertY` logic, and I need to be 100% certain it\'s right. Specifically, I\'m checking the math around `mouseDeltaY` and the resulting `aimCursorY`. I\'m verifying the right-hand rule and the sign of pitch and the ship. It needs to be perfect for all of the player\'s inputs.\n\n\n**Validating Y-Axis Control**\n\nI\'ve confirmed my intuition! If `invertY` is true, moving the mouse up causes the ship to pitch down \342\200\223 it\'s like pulling back on a joystick. The cursor always agrees with the ship\'s turn direction, which is super consistent and intuitive. I\'ve also verified the yaw sign: a mouse move to the right correctly yields a negative yaw angle, aligning perfectly with existing code.\n\n\n**Refining Rate Calculations**\n\nI\'m now deep in the rate calculations, and I\'ve built a curve from `aimCursorX`, `aimCursorY` to `targetYawRate` and `targetPitchRate`. This is going to be my perfect control response. I have a deadzone at the center for effortless straight flight and designed a smooth precision curve, with fine control near the center and full turn rates at the edges. I\'m blending linear and quadratic functions for this effect.\n\n\n**Optimizing Angular Control**\n\nI\'ve just perfected the target rate curve using a blend of linear and quadratic functions, ensuring smooth, precise control near the center with full rates at the edges. Now, I\'m integrating this with keyboard input, and clamping the combined results. Also, I am implementing smooth, rate-independent angular tracking by using lerp to maintain responsive movement. The result is a refined steering experience.\n\n\n**Analyzing Continuous Control**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on this continuous control behavior, frame by frame. When the mouse is at the screen edge, `aimCursorX` remains locked and unchanging, even with zero `mouseDeltaX`. This results in `targetYawRate` holding at its maximum value, causing continuous, full-speed turning. The same effect applies for `aimCursorY`, causing the ship to continually pitch. The issue is, how to get the ship to stop at a mouse-stopping-point (e.g. stop turning at a specific angle and not rotate forever).\n\n\n**Refining Stopping Logic**\n\nI\'m now diving into the stopping behavior to address the continuous turning. I\'m focusing on scenarios where the ship should halt at a specific angle when the mouse stops. I\'m investigating how to modify the control loop, or possibly the damping, to prevent perpetual rotation. My goal is to add a proper end-of-turn stop, so it doesn\'t rotate forever.\n\n\n**Investigating Gimbal Lock Behavior**\n\nI\'m now checking the behavior to prevent gimbal lock, and the logic in `CameraController.ts` with lag. Specifically, the smooth tracking of a full 360-degree vertical loop is achieved with no gimbal flip. With a constant `yawVelocity` at full speed, `lagX` smoothly holds steady as the camera orbits behind the turning ship, and there is no rollback. Now, I am switching to weapon aiming and the HUD reticle to achieve the best in-game experience.\n\n\n\n**Refining Screen Position**\n\nI\'ve just had a breakthrough. I realized the screen position is **DIRECTLY** tied to the persistent virtual mouse cursor values now. I\'ve uncovered a crucial error in `CombatManager.ts`. It was overwriting the `telemetry.aimScreenX` and `telemetry.aimScreenY` values with the lagged camera projection of the ship nose, causing the reticle to snap back to the center. This is resolved now. My focus is ensuring the persistent cursor is the only source.\n\n\n**Defining Weapon Convergence**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on defining weapon convergence, and how weapons should aim in relation to the boresight reticle and steering cursor. I\'ve realized that the ship\'s cannons should aim at the screen position of the virtual cursor (`aimScreenX`, `aimScreenY`), and that the ship\'s boresight reticle must always point to the boresight target. This ensures visual consistency and gives pilots direct steering feedback.\n\n\n**Defining Weapon Targeting**\n\nI am now focusing on refining weapon convergence and determining how weapons should target relative to the boresight reticle and steering cursor. With this logic, ship cannons will precisely target the screen position of the virtual cursor (aimScreenX, aimScreenY). Concurrently, the ship\'s boresight reticle must consistently point toward the boresight target, ensuring a visually consistent and intuitive steering feedback system for the pilot.\n\n\n**Refining Cannon Targeting**\n\nI\'m now integrating weapon aiming logic. I realized that `CombatManager.firePlayerWeapon()` already calls `flight.getAimTargetPoint()`.  The guns should get a small gimbal deflection proportional to cursor position (clamped to ~9 degrees), and I\'m ensuring the boresight marker shows the weapon convergence point, while the virtual cursor displays the persistent mouse position, including edge positions.\n\n\n**Verifying Pointer Lock Control**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the pointer lock behavior. When active, relative mouse movements update the `aimCursorX` and `aimCursorY` smoothly. At the edges, the cursor persistently holds, and the ship continuously turns. The browser and the iframe `<iframe>` both perform as expected. This persistent behavior continues.\n\n\n**Validating Pause/Resume Logic**\n\nNow I\'m completely focused on pause/resume lifecycle events and how they affect the persistent cursor. Specifically, I\'ve confirmed that `flight.reset()` *only* triggers on mission start or restart, zeroing the cursor. Crucially, I\'m ensuring `centerAimCursor()` *only* runs on `handleResume()`, not during gameplay. This eliminates any unwanted ship movement when resuming from the pause menu, preserving the player\'s intentional control input.\n\n\n**Confirming Mouse Edge Handling**\n\nI\'ve just validated the edge behavior. Even when moving fast to an edge, and the physical mouse stops, the math keeps `aimCursorX` and `aimCursorY` persistently pegged at the screen edge. This means no undesired decay and the cursor remains locked in place. At `mouseSensitivity = 1.0`, moving the mouse right 400px moves `aimCursorX` to +0.952, hitting the screen\'s right edge, and is then clamped to +0.96.\n\n\n**Validating Persistent Control**\n\nI\'m thrilled with this continuous, persistent control! I confirmed smooth turning, looping, and pitching with zero interruption or rollback at full speed when the cursor is at an edge.  Moving the mouse from the edge to center smoothly slows and stops the turn. I can now perform continuous diagonal banking turns. I validated sensitivity, max speed, and boost - no changes.\n\n\n**Validating System Integrity**\n\nI\'ve just confirmed the current changes do not impact any other systems. The new aiming retains original `A/D` roll, respects mouse sensitivity and Y-axis inversion, and completely preserves camera smoothing and distance settings. Additionally, it leaves combat, weapons, enemy AI, and all missions untouched.\n\n\n**Finalizing Mouse Steering**\n\nI\'ve got this locked down! The virtual mouse steering cursor values, `aimCursorX` and `aimCursorY`, are now consistently maintained within the range `[-0.96, +0.96] x [-0.94, +0.94]`. Auto-recentering, rollback, and decay are gone. A deadzone and precision nonlinear curve drive continuous turning and pitch. I\'ve also implemented forward weapon gimbal deflection for enhanced shot tracking during sharp maneuvers. I\'m now writing the telemetry data from the cursor, directly.\n\n\n**Integrating HUD Feedback**\n\nI\'m now integrating the HUD feedback, rendering both the persistent mouse steering cursor and the ship\'s cannon boresight. I\'m rendering the steering cursor directly at its calculated screen position. Also, the ship boresight indicator will be rendered, so the pilot knows about cannon alignment. Finally, a subtle steering vector tether is added for responsiveness.\n\n\n"
