  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Flight Feel Restoration & Mouse Aim Boundary Tuning\n\n## Current Situation\n\nThe mouse boundary issue has finally been fixed.\n\nHowever, the latest fix introduced two new problems:\n\n1. Flight handling feels significantly worse than before.\n2. Mouse aiming has no effective gameplay boundary. The aiming position can move all the way to the extreme edges of the screen.\n\nThe previous flight prototype had a much better overall feel. Preserve that original handling wherever possible.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nRestore the original responsive, smooth arcade flight experience while retaining the successful mouse input fix.\n\nDo not reintroduce the previous mouse edge rollback problem.\n\n## 1. Preserve the Successful Mouse Input Fix\n\n- Inspect the exact changes that fixed the mouse boundary rollback.\n- Preserve the working Pointer Lock / relative mouse input implementation.\n- Continue using `movementX` and `movementY` for relative mouse input.\n- Do not introduce cursor recentering.\n- Do not depend on absolute screen coordinates.\n- Verify that continuous mouse movement works even when the physical mouse reaches the edge of the display.\n\nDo not blindly rewrite the input system.\n\n## 2. Restore Flight Handling\n\nInspect the current flight controller and compare it with the original Phase 1 implementation or available Git history.\n\nInvestigate changes to:\n\n- Mouse sensitivity\n- Yaw and pitch response\n- Flight acceleration\n- Angular acceleration\n- Rotation speed limits\n- Inertia and damping\n- Input smoothing\n- Camera follow behavior\n- Delta-time handling\n\nRestore the original handling characteristics where they were changed unintentionally.\n\nThe target feel:\n\n- Responsive and predictable mouse steering.\n- Smooth rotation without excessive delay.\n- Controlled inertia.\n- No sudden snapping or unwanted drifting.\n- Smooth third-person chase camera.\n- Boost should retain its original acceleration and visual feedback.\n- Flight should feel consistent at different frame rates.\n\nDo not arbitrarily reduce sensitivity or add excessive smoothing.\n\n## 3. Implement a Bounded Virtual Aim Region\n\nThe current aiming range is too large.\n\nImplement a virtual aiming region independent of the physical mouse position.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Define configurable horizontal and vertical aiming limits.\n- Keep the virtual reticle within a reasonable region of the screen.\n- Clamp accumulated aiming offset to those limits.\n- Make the boundary smooth and predictable.\n- Avoid sudden snapping when reaching or leaving the boundary.\n- Keep the flight controller responsive near the boundary.\n- Allow the ship to continue turning even when the reticle reaches its limit.\n\nImportant: The virtual aiming boundary must not disable or interrupt relative mouse input.\n\nDo not use physical cursor coordinates to implement this boundary.\n\n## 4. Camera and Flight Integration\n\nCheck whether camera movement and ship rotation are being driven by conflicting input systems.\n\nEnsure:\n\n- Mouse input is processed only once.\n- Flight controller owns ship rotation.\n- Camera controller follows the ship smoothly.\n- Camera smoothing does not create excessive input latency.\n- Aim offset and ship rotation use clearly separated responsibilities.\n\n## 5. Testing\n\nTest the actual browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Mouse movement remains continuous at all physical screen edges.\n2. The virtual reticle stays inside its configured aiming region.\n3. The ship continues responding when the reticle reaches the boundary.\n4. Normal steering feels responsive and smooth.\n5. Camera follow remains stable.\n6. Boost behavior remains intact.\n7. No regression to the previously fixed mouse rollback issue.\n\nCompare the result with the original Phase 1 flight feel whenever the original implementation is available.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Do not redesign the game.\n- Do not change unrelated combat, bot AI, mission, or UI systems.\n- Do not replace the successful Pointer Lock fix.\n- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n- Do not claim success without testing the actual browser game.\n\n## Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Why flight handling became worse.\n2. Which changes caused the regression.\n3. How the virtual aiming boundary works.\n4. Which original flight characteristics were restored.\n5. Actual browser testing results.\n6. Any remaining issues.\n\nPrioritize restoring flight feel and fixing the aiming boundary without breaking continuous mouse input."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Flight Feel Restoration & Mouse Aim Boundary Tuning\n\n## Current Situation\n\nThe mouse boundary issue has finally been fixed.\n\nHowever, the latest fix introduced two new problems:\n\n1. Flight handling feels significantly worse than before.\n2. Mouse aiming has no effective gameplay boundary. The aiming position can move all the way to the extreme edges of the screen.\n\nThe previous flight prototype had a much better overall feel. Preserve that original handling wherever possible.\n\n## Primary Objective\n\nRestore the original responsive, smooth arcade flight experience while retaining the successful mouse input fix.\n\nDo not reintroduce the previous mouse edge rollback problem.\n\n## 1. Preserve the Successful Mouse Input Fix\n\n- Inspect the exact changes that fixed the mouse boundary rollback.\n- Preserve the working Pointer Lock / relative mouse input implementation.\n- Continue using `movementX` and `movementY` for relative mouse input.\n- Do not introduce cursor recentering.\n- Do not depend on absolute screen coordinates.\n- Verify that continuous mouse movement works even when the physical mouse reaches the edge of the display.\n\nDo not blindly rewrite the input system.\n\n## 2. Restore Flight Handling\n\nInspect the current flight controller and compare it with the original Phase 1 implementation or available Git history.\n\nInvestigate changes to:\n\n- Mouse sensitivity\n- Yaw and pitch response\n- Flight acceleration\n- Angular acceleration\n- Rotation speed limits\n- Inertia and damping\n- Input smoothing\n- Camera follow behavior\n- Delta-time handling\n\nRestore the original handling characteristics where they were changed unintentionally.\n\nThe target feel:\n\n- Responsive and predictable mouse steering.\n- Smooth rotation without excessive delay.\n- Controlled inertia.\n- No sudden snapping or unwanted drifting.\n- Smooth third-person chase camera.\n- Boost should retain its original acceleration and visual feedback.\n- Flight should feel consistent at different frame rates.\n\nDo not arbitrarily reduce sensitivity or add excessive smoothing.\n\n## 3. Implement a Bounded Virtual Aim Region\n\nThe current aiming range is too large.\n\nImplement a virtual aiming region independent of the physical mouse position.\n\nRequirements:\n\n- Define configurable horizontal and vertical aiming limits.\n- Keep the virtual reticle within a reasonable region of the screen.\n- Clamp accumulated aiming offset to those limits.\n- Make the boundary smooth and predictable.\n- Avoid sudden snapping when reaching or leaving the boundary.\n- Keep the flight controller responsive near the boundary.\n- Allow the ship to continue turning even when the reticle reaches its limit.\n\nImportant: The virtual aiming boundary must not disable or interrupt relative mouse input.\n\nDo not use physical cursor coordinates to implement this boundary.\n\n## 4. Camera and Flight Integration\n\nCheck whether camera movement and ship rotation are being driven by conflicting input systems.\n\nEnsure:\n\n- Mouse input is processed only once.\n- Flight controller owns ship rotation.\n- Camera controller follows the ship smoothly.\n- Camera smoothing does not create excessive input latency.\n- Aim offset and ship rotation use clearly separated responsibilities.\n\n## 5. Testing\n\nTest the actual browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Mouse movement remains continuous at all physical screen edges.\n2. The virtual reticle stays inside its configured aiming region.\n3. The ship continues responding when the reticle reaches the boundary.\n4. Normal steering feels responsive and smooth.\n5. Camera follow remains stable.\n6. Boost behavior remains intact.\n7. No regression to the previously fixed mouse rollback issue.\n\nCompare the result with the original Phase 1 flight feel whenever the original implementation is available.\n\n## Constraints\n\n- Do not redesign the game.\n- Do not change unrelated combat, bot AI, mission, or UI systems.\n- Do not replace the successful Pointer Lock fix.\n- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.\n- Do not claim success without testing the actual browser game.\n\n## Final Report\n\nReport:\n\n1. Why flight handling became worse.\n2. Which changes caused the regression.\n3. How the virtual aiming boundary works.\n4. Which original flight characteristics were restored.\n5. Actual browser testing results.\n6. Any remaining issues.\n\nPrioritize restoring flight feel and fixing the aiming boundary without breaking continuous mouse input."
    }
