  user_input {
    user_response: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Progressive Mouse Steering & Sensitivity Tuning\n\nImplement a configurable progressive mouse steering system.\n\n## Desired Player Experience\n\nI want a mouse steering system with three distinct behavior zones:\n\n1. Precision Zone:\n   - Inside the normal aiming boundary.\n   - Precise and predictable steering.\n   - Normal mouse sensitivity.\n\n2. Transition Zone:\n   - When the virtual reticle first reaches and passes the aiming boundary.\n   - Steering initially becomes slower.\n   - As the player continues moving the physical mouse farther in the same direction, steering speed progressively increases.\n\n3. Acceleration Zone:\n   - Continued mouse movement beyond the boundary produces increasingly faster ship rotation.\n   - The player can perform fast turns without hitting a hard steering limit.\n   - The acceleration must remain smooth and controllable.\n\n## Mathematical Model\n\nUse accumulated relative mouse displacement to maintain a virtual aim position.\n\nLet:\n\n- x = virtual aim displacement on one axis.\n- B = normal aiming boundary.\n- S = acceleration distance scale.\n- e = max(0, (abs(x) - B) / S).\n\nUse a configurable nonlinear response curve.\n\nA suitable initial model is:\n\nf(e) = e^p / (1 + e^p)\n\nThen:\n\nsensitivity(e) = boundarySensitivity + (maxSensitivity - boundarySensitivity) * f(e)\n\nSuggested initial tuning values:\n\n- Normal sensitivity: 1.0\n- Boundary sensitivity: 0.35\n- Maximum sensitivity multiplier: 2.5\n- Curve exponent p: 2.0\n- Acceleration distance scale: configurable\n- Recovery smoothing: approximately 0.12 seconds\n\nThese are starting values, not fixed final values.\n\nInside the precision zone, preserve normal sensitivity.\n\nAt the boundary, transition smoothly toward the lower boundary sensitivity.\n\nOutside the boundary, increase sensitivity progressively according to the response curve.\n\nAvoid sudden sensitivity jumps.\n\n## Input Requirements\n\n- Preserve the successfully fixed Pointer Lock implementation.\n- Continue using movementX and movementY.\n- Never depend on absolute cursor coordinates.\n- Never introduce cursor recentering.\n- Mouse input must remain continuous at physical screen edges.\n- Accumulate relative displacement correctly.\n- Handle horizontal and vertical steering independently.\n- Prevent input from being processed twice.\n- Use delta time for time-dependent smoothing.\n\n## Flight Controller Integration\n\n- Keep mouse steering separate from camera follow.\n- Keep ship rotation responsive.\n- Preserve the original Phase 1 flight characteristics wherever possible.\n- Do not add excessive inertia or smoothing.\n- Ensure the transition between precision aiming and accelerated turning feels natural.\n- Avoid uncontrollable rotation at maximum sensitivity.\n\n## Player Settings\n\nAdd configurable sensitivity settings:\n\n- Base Sensitivity\n- Boundary Sensitivity\n- Maximum Sensitivity\n- Acceleration Distance\n- Response Curve\n- Aim Boundary Size\n\nIf appropriate, expose them in the Settings UI.\n\nThe player should be able to tune the system without changing source code.\n\n## Testing\n\nTest all three zones in the actual browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Precise steering inside the aiming boundary.\n2. Slower steering immediately after crossing the boundary.\n3. Increasing steering speed with continued outward mouse movement.\n4. Smooth return to precision steering.\n5. Continuous input at physical screen edges.\n6. No sudden rotation spikes.\n7. Stable behavior at different frame rates.\n\nTune the curve based on actual gameplay testing.\n\nDo not change unrelated game systems.\n\n## Final Report\n\nExplain the implemented mathematical response curve, the final tuning values, and the browser test results. Clearly identify any remaining issues."
    items {
      text: "# STARFALL \342\200\224 Progressive Mouse Steering & Sensitivity Tuning\n\nImplement a configurable progressive mouse steering system.\n\n## Desired Player Experience\n\nI want a mouse steering system with three distinct behavior zones:\n\n1. Precision Zone:\n   - Inside the normal aiming boundary.\n   - Precise and predictable steering.\n   - Normal mouse sensitivity.\n\n2. Transition Zone:\n   - When the virtual reticle first reaches and passes the aiming boundary.\n   - Steering initially becomes slower.\n   - As the player continues moving the physical mouse farther in the same direction, steering speed progressively increases.\n\n3. Acceleration Zone:\n   - Continued mouse movement beyond the boundary produces increasingly faster ship rotation.\n   - The player can perform fast turns without hitting a hard steering limit.\n   - The acceleration must remain smooth and controllable.\n\n## Mathematical Model\n\nUse accumulated relative mouse displacement to maintain a virtual aim position.\n\nLet:\n\n- x = virtual aim displacement on one axis.\n- B = normal aiming boundary.\n- S = acceleration distance scale.\n- e = max(0, (abs(x) - B) / S).\n\nUse a configurable nonlinear response curve.\n\nA suitable initial model is:\n\nf(e) = e^p / (1 + e^p)\n\nThen:\n\nsensitivity(e) = boundarySensitivity + (maxSensitivity - boundarySensitivity) * f(e)\n\nSuggested initial tuning values:\n\n- Normal sensitivity: 1.0\n- Boundary sensitivity: 0.35\n- Maximum sensitivity multiplier: 2.5\n- Curve exponent p: 2.0\n- Acceleration distance scale: configurable\n- Recovery smoothing: approximately 0.12 seconds\n\nThese are starting values, not fixed final values.\n\nInside the precision zone, preserve normal sensitivity.\n\nAt the boundary, transition smoothly toward the lower boundary sensitivity.\n\nOutside the boundary, increase sensitivity progressively according to the response curve.\n\nAvoid sudden sensitivity jumps.\n\n## Input Requirements\n\n- Preserve the successfully fixed Pointer Lock implementation.\n- Continue using movementX and movementY.\n- Never depend on absolute cursor coordinates.\n- Never introduce cursor recentering.\n- Mouse input must remain continuous at physical screen edges.\n- Accumulate relative displacement correctly.\n- Handle horizontal and vertical steering independently.\n- Prevent input from being processed twice.\n- Use delta time for time-dependent smoothing.\n\n## Flight Controller Integration\n\n- Keep mouse steering separate from camera follow.\n- Keep ship rotation responsive.\n- Preserve the original Phase 1 flight characteristics wherever possible.\n- Do not add excessive inertia or smoothing.\n- Ensure the transition between precision aiming and accelerated turning feels natural.\n- Avoid uncontrollable rotation at maximum sensitivity.\n\n## Player Settings\n\nAdd configurable sensitivity settings:\n\n- Base Sensitivity\n- Boundary Sensitivity\n- Maximum Sensitivity\n- Acceleration Distance\n- Response Curve\n- Aim Boundary Size\n\nIf appropriate, expose them in the Settings UI.\n\nThe player should be able to tune the system without changing source code.\n\n## Testing\n\nTest all three zones in the actual browser game.\n\nVerify:\n\n1. Precise steering inside the aiming boundary.\n2. Slower steering immediately after crossing the boundary.\n3. Increasing steering speed with continued outward mouse movement.\n4. Smooth return to precision steering.\n5. Continuous input at physical screen edges.\n6. No sudden rotation spikes.\n7. Stable behavior at different frame rates.\n\nTune the curve based on actual gameplay testing.\n\nDo not change unrelated game systems.\n\n## Final Report\n\nExplain the implemented mathematical response curve, the final tuning values, and the browser test results. Clearly identify any remaining issues."
    }
  }
}
