  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyze System Design**\n\nI\'m deep in the design phase, meticulously analyzing the user\'s requirements. My focus is on achieving a mathematically sound, smooth, and responsive progressive mouse steering system. I am working to ensure it\'s easy to control, doesn\'t break Pointer Lock, and ultimately, is fun to use!\n\n\n**Refine Parameter Interactions**\n\nI\'m now deeply considering the interactions between x, B, S, sensitivity(e), recovery smoothing, and ship rotation rate. My thoughts have zeroed in on the physics and math of how the parameters influence the user\'s experience. Making the parameter settings clean and intuitive is paramount!\n\n\n**Define Precision Zone**\n\nI\'m now focusing on the Precision Zone. The goal is precise, predictable steering within the $|x| \\le B$ boundary, using base sensitivity. I\'m scrutinizing the mathematical transition at the boundary. I want to ensure smooth transitions and avoid any abrupt sensitivity jumps. My current thinking revolves around normalizing the aim and tracking accumulated mouse displacement per axis.\n\n\n**Investigate Excess Zone**\n\nI\'m now grappling with the edge case: what happens when the user overshoots the `B` boundary and needs to return to the precision zone? I\'m testing a soft upper bound on `virtualAimX/Y` to prevent excessive mouse travel for return. A maximum excess ratio of 4.5 or 5.0 feels right. I\'m also considering responsive inward return mechanics when the player actively pulls inward. I need to clarify the prompt\'s \"Recovery smoothing: approximately 0.12 seconds\" detail.\n\n\n**Clarify Sensitivity Logic**\n\nI\'m now clarifying the sensitivity logic to align with the suggested tuning values. Specifically, the normal sensitivity is 1.0 and boundary sensitivity is 0.35. The maximum multiplier is 2.5, and the curve exponent `p` is 2.0. I\'m focusing on preserving normal sensitivity inside the precision zone and transitioning smoothly to the boundary sensitivity. My thoughts are centering on the importance of avoiding abrupt sensitivity jumps, especially at the boundary. I want to guarantee a smooth experience.\n\n\n**Smooth Sensitivity Transition**\n\nI\'m now focused on achieving a smooth sensitivity transition at the boundary. I\'m exploring a spatial blend band near $|x| = B$, smoothly transitioning from base to boundary sensitivity. I like the idea of using BOTH: a spatial smoothstep AND a delta-time exponential smoothing filter for a truly smooth experience. I am analyzing the pros and cons of these options!\n\n\n**Confirm Sensitivity Effect**\n\nI\'ve got a breakthrough! I\'ve confirmed `sensitivity(e)` multiplies the steering directly! The implementation directly implements \"Recovery smoothing: approximately 0.12 seconds\" and guarantees smooth return to precision steering and zero sudden rotation spikes. I\'m now making sure the system satisfies EVERY requirement of the prompt.\n\n\n**Evaluate Acceleration Dynamics**\n\nMy focus has shifted to the Acceleration Zone. I\'m now zeroing in on how ship rotation speed, or yaw/pitch rate, is affected by mouse displacement. My goal is to ensure smooth, controllable acceleration, avoiding any hard steering limits. I\'m considering how `sensitivity(e)` affects acceleration in this region. I\'m exploring Interpretation A\'s effects on the control scheme, specifically, the responsiveness of the virtual aim and turn rates in response to mouse displacement.\n\n\n**Quantify Control Scheme**\n\nI\'m now quantifying the control scheme\'s behavior at key boundary distances. My immediate concern is establishing precise ship rotation rates at $|x| = 0.5B$, $|x| = 1.0B$, $|x| = 1.1B (e=0.1)$, $|x| = 2.0B (e=1.0)$, and $|x| = 4.0B (e=3.0)$. I\'m re-reading the Transition Zone details, clarifying the relationship between physical mouse movement and ship steering speed to build an intuitive, dynamic control.\n\n\n**Calculate Turn Rate**\n\nI\'m now calculating turn rates to validate behavior across the Precision, Transition, and Excess Zones. I\'m focusing on the impact of `sensitivity(e)` and the rate of change of turn rate per unit of displacement (`d(turnRate)/dx`). My priority is to model and compare the two ways sensitivity drives turn rate. The plan is to clearly map the relationship between mouse movement and ship steering speed to confirm the control scheme\'s intended dynamic.\n\n\n**Compare Steering Models**\n\nI\'m now comparing and contrasting two steering models, analyzing their adherence to the prompt\'s specifications. The initial focus is evaluating Model 1, which incorporates immediate sensitivity adjustments, versus Model 2\'s incremental steering rate approach. I\'m carefully re-reading the prompt\'s details to assess both models, focusing on precision, predictability, and boundary behavior.\n\n\n**Validate Steering Response**\n\nI\'m now validating the overall steering response across all zones, paying close attention to the smooth acceleration as described in the prompt. I am modeling the ship\'s turn rate per unit of displacement, and analyzing it for smoothness. I want to build an intuitive, responsive control scheme, particularly as the physical mouse moves farther in the same direction, to ensure progressive increase in speed.\n\n\n**Compare Zone Sensitivity**\n\nI am now thoroughly comparing sensitivity in the zones described. I\'m focusing on the contrast between direct steering sensitivity (Model 1) and integrated rate (Model 2) under the given conditions. I\'m focusing on \"Slower steering immediately after crossing the boundary\" and the player continuously moving the mouse to the right. My goal is to determine the best model.\n\n\n**Refine Boundary Crossing**\n\nI\'m now refining the boundary crossing, focusing on the sensitivity curve\'s unexpected behavior. I am noticing that sensitivity dips *below* normal at the boundary, rising again as displacement increases. I am adjusting the mathematical formulas and parameter values to create a smoother, more predictable transition where the `sensitivity` never drops below 1.0. This is the new focus for validation.\n\n\n**Evaluate Sensitivity Transition**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the numbers! I am calculating how the sensitivity changes at different displacement levels. I\'m scrutinizing the impact of the formula and how the values translate to actual steering responses. I want to see how these numerical results align with the requested player experience in each zone.\n\n\n**Verify Asymptotic Behavior**\n\nI\'m now zeroing in on sensitivity\'s asymptotic behavior as displacement increases towards infinity. I see the formula\'s smooth, controllable limit at a sensitivity of 2.5, as required. My analysis has shifted to validating the continuous turn rate and steering feel across the axis. I\'m focusing on independent per-axis virtual aim displacements and applying a generous soft cap to prevent excessive travel when reversing.\n\n\n**Refine Reversal Mechanics**\n\nI\'m now refining the reversal mechanics within the Excess Zone. Specifically, when the user reverses direction from deep in the zone, I\'m focusing on the virtual aim\'s behavior. I\'m exploring an inward return multiplier that smoothly pulls `x` back faster during inward mouse movement. This approach should feel more responsive and intuitive. I\'m carefully evaluating the formula for this multiplier to achieve a good balance!\n\n\n**Validate Reversal Behavior**\n\nI\'m now carefully validating the reversal behavior within the Excess Zone. My focus is on ensuring smooth transitions and responsiveness when the user changes mouse direction. I need to make sure the virtual aim behaves intuitively and returns to the precision zone as expected. I\'m focusing specifically on the impact of various return multipliers on overall feel.\n\n\n**Verify Asymptotic Behavior**\n\nI\'ve validated the exact per-axis sensitivity and turn-rate across all three zones and now have a firm understanding. With the boundary at `B`, I am finding the max excess distance `S`. Then, the formula shows that a maximum excess ratio of 4.0 delivers the target player experience. Sensitivity is very crisp to reach and quick to return from, with accurate 1:1 mouse tracking.\n\n\n**Confirm Parameter Effects**\n\nI\'m now focusing on how the computed boundary pixels `B` and sensitivity values interact. I\'m exploring how widening the aim boundary proportionally increases precision space. I want to ensure the visual boundary directly informs the control scheme\'s responsiveness. I am looking closely at target sensitivity.\n\n\n**Validate Smoothness**\n\nI\'m now validating the smoothness of the entire system, focusing specifically on angular turn speed. I am verifying that the sensitivity formula guarantees a $C^1$-smooth transition at the boundary. Then, I\'m working to verify how the recovery smoothing directly translates to a smooth, intuitive player experience.\n\n\n**Refine Deadzone Behavior**\n\nI\'m now refining the deadzone behavior and calculating turn multipliers numerically for default settings. I\'m focusing on the `uEff` calculation after the deadzone. I\'m validating the relationship between mouse displacement, turn magnitude, and sensitivity in the precision zone. I want to guarantee a clear, intuitive feel. I\'ll check `precisionMag`\'s contribution, and calculate `dirMag`, and `targetSens` to get `turnMultiplier`.\n\n\n**Calculate Turn Rate**\n\nI\'m now calculating turn rates to validate the model\'s performance under various conditions, expanding from default settings. I am testing how target sensitivity influences yaw speed across the three zones. I\'ve focused on various positions and mouse speeds. I\'ve got good values!\n\n\n**Validate Steering Parameters**\n\nI\'m now validating the derived parameter values, especially `B` and sensitivity, against the requirements. I am confident that the sensitivity values smoothly translate to a high-quality player experience. I\'m focusing on the relationship between visual boundary and control responsiveness. I am checking the calculated turn rates to confirm the behavior meets expectations.\n\n\n**Confirm Steering Speed**\n\nMy focus now is on confirming the increase in steering speed based on mouse movement. I\'m verifying the system\'s behavior, specifically the progressive increase in steering speed as the mouse moves further in the same direction. I am pleased with how the values are translating.\n\n\n**Refine Zone Independence**\n\nI\'m now implementing independent horizontal and vertical axes! I\'ve confirmed that yaw and pitch are now separate, which ensures horizontal acceleration doesn\'t impact vertical precision. This is a big win for control. I am also working on reticle movement within the boundary!\n\n\n**Visualize Zone Indicators**\n\nI\'m now thinking about visual cues to improve the player\'s understanding of the zones. I\'m exploring representing the boundary with a visual indicator, possibly using subtle corner brackets or a reticle tint. This aims to provide instant feedback on steering zones without adding clutter. The visual effect should scale with the viewport\'s aspect ratio!\n\n\n**Define Aim Boundary**\n\nI\'m now defining the relationship between `aimBoundarySize` and `B`, the virtual aiming boundary in pixels. My current approach involves mapping the normalized screen fraction to a pixel value, ensuring the visual boundary directly informs the control scheme. I\'ve calculated the appropriate formulas, allowing the player to customize the precision zone\'s size. I am finding this system intuitive and natural!\n\n\n**Implement Parameter Ranges**\n\nI\'ve successfully implemented player-adjustable settings! The boundary and visual reticle now scale responsively and proportionally to each other. I\'ve re-read the prompt and added player-adjustable Base Sensitivity, Boundary Sensitivity, Maximum Sensitivity, and Acceleration Distance.\n\n\n**Verify Parameter Scaling**\n\nI\'m now verifying that all player-adjustable parameters, like boundary size and sensitivity values, scale as expected. I\'m confirming the formula, visual reticle, and responsiveness. I\'ve re-read the prompt to make sure it is all working as intended!\n\n\n**Evaluate Recovery Smoothing**\n\nI\'m now carefully evaluating the `0.12s` recovery smoothing. I\'ve implemented the code, tracing transitions. My calculations demonstrate smooth sensitivity adjustments that are frame-rate independent, ensuring zero sudden rotation spikes. This should result in intuitive and enjoyable player control.\n\n\n**Confirm Reset State**\n\nI\'m now focusing on validating the system reset state, specifically ensuring `virtualAimX/Y` are reset to zero upon mission restart or pause-resume. This will guarantee a consistent starting point for player control and intuitive behavior. I\'m confirming that the system handles telemetry and camera interactions correctly.\n\n\n"
